{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297074532,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-bf89a8d634eddd67","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-http-query-sugar-research/plans/board.yaml","offset":195,"limit":40},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-http-query-sugar-research/plans/board.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n195:   title: 'Research spike (prelude-partials-and-mutation round, Q3): maintainer answer -- both struct-like and array-like partial application are needed, chosen by the input type''s shape, and true currying should fall out for free once closures/functions are first-class rather than being its own mechanism. Look at how Rust and similar languages simplify this before a final design; write findings to plans/'\n196:   kind: build\n197:   needs: []\n198:   prio: 3\n199:   status: todo\n200: \n201: - id: http-query-sugar-research\n202:   title: 'Maintainer note (docs/.annotations/notes.json composed 2026-08-31T22:08:16Z, unscheduled): consider a simple HTTP query builtin (like Python requests) as thin sugar over whatever each backend runtime already provides, minimal added complexity. Research spike: survey what request/response building blocks already exist per backend (Go net/http, JS fetch, Python urllib, Lua, Rust reqwest, native) before scoping a design row. Write findings to plans/.'\n203:   kind: build\n204:   needs: []\n205:   prio: 5\n206:   status: todo\n207: \n208: - id: partial-application-system-design\n209:   issue: gh:119\n210:   title: 'Grill array-like vs struct-like partial application for the colon method-call operator (gh:119 ruling spinoff), informed by closures-first-class-functions-research -- maintainer: \"either five_plus(1) is correct (but still becomes curried) or five_plus [1, 2] is correct; also function will still always only have one parameter; a true curried function would be a function that returns another function; we may want to implement this, or perhaps consider instead thinking about formalizing at least the beginning of a partial application system which could either apply a partial array-like or a partial struct-like\"'\n211:   kind: decide\n212:   needs: [closures-first-class-functions-research]\n213:   status: todo\n214: \n215: \n216: \n217: - id: draft-core-model-migration\n218:   title: Migrate the two-layer/cardinality core model to a concepts guide; embedded open threads go to the tracker (draft-split)\n219:   kind: build\n220:   model: fable\n221:   needs: []\n222:   soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n223:   prio: 4\n224:   status: todo\n225: \n226: - id: draft-access-model-migration\n227:   title: Verify unary functions, lens access, and the dimension-spec model against the reference pages; salvage rationale, delete sections (draft-split)\n228:   kind: build\n229:   needs: []\n230:   soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n231:   prio: 4\n232:   status: todo\n233: \n234: - id: draft-numbers-operators-migration\n\n(Showing lines 195-234 of 378. Use offset=235 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"  title: 'Research spike (prelude-partials-and-mutation round, Q3): maintainer answer -- both struct-like and array-like partial application are needed, chosen by the input type''s shape, and true currying should fall out for free once closures/functions are first-class rather than being its own mechanism. Look at how Rust and similar languages simplify this before a final design; write findings to plans/'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: http-query-sugar-research\n  title: 'Maintainer note (docs/.annotations/notes.json composed 2026-08-31T22:08:16Z, unscheduled): consider a simple HTTP query builtin (like Python requests) as thin sugar over whatever each backend runtime already provides, minimal added complexity. Research spike: survey what request/response building blocks already exist per backend (Go net/http, JS fetch, Python urllib, Lua, Rust reqwest, native) before scoping a design row. Write findings to plans/.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 5\n  status: todo\n\n- id: partial-application-system-design\n  issue: gh:119\n  title: 'Grill array-like vs struct-like partial application for the colon method-call operator (gh:119 ruling spinoff), informed by closures-first-class-functions-research -- maintainer: \"either five_plus(1) is correct (but still becomes curried) or five_plus [1, 2] is correct; also function will still always only have one parameter; a true curried function would be a function that returns another function; we may want to implement this, or perhaps consider instead thinking about formalizing at least the beginning of a partial application system which could either apply a partial array-like or a partial struct-like\"'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [closures-first-class-functions-research]\n  status: todo\n","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-http-query-sugar-research/plans/board.yaml","text":"  title: 'Research spike (prelude-partials-and-mutation round, Q3): maintainer answer -- both struct-like and array-like partial application are needed, chosen by the input type''s shape, and true currying should fall out for free once closures/functions are first-class rather than being its own mechanism. Look at how Rust and similar languages simplify this before a final design; write findings to plans/'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: http-query-sugar-research\n  title: 'Maintainer note (docs/.annotations/notes.json composed 2026-08-31T22:08:16Z, unscheduled): consider a simple HTTP query builtin (like Python requests) as thin sugar over whatever each backend runtime already provides, minimal added complexity. Research spike: survey what request/response building blocks already exist p