{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297085327,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8d5d9a898f0ef7d6","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -rn -i \"stream\\|effect\\|input\\|output\\|sink\\|source\" draft.md | head -60"},"output":"18:**Effect layer** means multiplicity is a property of *evaluation*. An expression that yields\n19:three things is not a container; it produced three results. The name is borrowed from effect\n20:systems, where an effect describes *how* an expression evaluates rather than what it returns.\n28:jq's defining feature is that every expression produces a *stream* of 0..n values. The insight\n29:this design rests on is that a stream and a collection are the same information stored in two\n44:.[]       value  -> effect      # reflect\n45:[ ... ]   effect -> value       # reify\n48:Everything else stays in its layer. `select`, `..`, `,` and `//` live in the effect layer;\n64:If multiplicity in the effect layer is purely an evaluation-time phenomenon, with no stream\n65:*values* existing at all, then the two distinctions collapse into one. The effect layer is then\n68:**Chosen: the collapse, with a spelling.** Multiplicity in the effect layer is\n69:evaluation-level, and `Stream<T>` is its type -- the type of an expression that yields its\n70:entries as evaluation proceeds, not the type of a stream object. The cardinality table below\n72:of this document read as contradicting each other here. The alternative, streams as\n73:first-class values, would have meant three things to track (`Vec`, a `Stream` value, and\n74:effect multiplicity) and a held value of genuinely unknown extent. See\n75:[the decision](#decided-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed) for the full rules.\n83:Stream<T>    0..n, possibly infinite, indexing only by iteration\n86:`Vec` and `Stream` are not \"eager\" and \"lazy\". The honest difference is the **promise they\n87:make**. `Vec` guarantees indexing without iteration; `Stream` does not, and may be infinite.\n91:the foo[] operator is actually just applying another vector as a projection on a vector or a stream, so that would be\n94:The filter `.[3]` would be getting the third item of each value in the stream. If a 2D vector is projected by a vector of length one, then you'd get not single items, but a one dimensional vector.\n97:memory and rather than beocming streams since they have fixed cardinality, even if that si also only derived from their \"parent\" object. This means that even `.[]` only becomes a stream if `.` was already a stream, I guess. but when to materialize is another question: i guess that `.[] | select ...etc ...` would be allowed to materialiez teh resutl instead of making it a lens, even tho it could appear like a lens in the type system, that is if we can assume that no more references to `.` exist elsewhere.\n106:thing that turns a value into effect-layer multiplicity. Under projection-with-lenses, `.[]` on\n110:the effect layer would then arise only from genuinely unknown cardinality (I/O, `select`, `..`),\n124:effect-layer multiplicity at all. What does? Only things with genuinely unknown extent: I/O,\n130:Keep pulling and the effect layer shrinks to one thing: genuine streaming input whose length is\n133:than two, running one way, `[...]` collapsing a stream into a value.\n136:`Stream[3]` consumes three items and cannot yield a view. Different cost, different guarantee,\n154:**Third option: there is no value-to-effect shifter, because it would only destroy\n155:information.** Ask what converting a `Vec` into effect multiplicity actually buys. Effect-layer\n160:Under this reading, effect multiplicity is *born* from streaming sources whose length has not\n166:born effect, collapsed to a value. Writing a `Vec` to stdout iterates internally and needs no\n167:type-level stream. Avoiding an intermediate materialization is fusion, which is a compiler\n172:It answers [the streams question](plans/questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity). If effect multiplicity is only ever born from I/O and only ever collapses into\n173:values, there is no need for a `Stream<T>` *type* at all. `Stream` becomes an effect annotation\n182:first-class `Stream` value would be, so [the streams question](plans/questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity) and this proposal stand or fall together.\n202:bitmap            1 bit per SOURCE element.  Cheap to AND together.  Indexing needs rank.\n217:   proportional to survivors rather than to sources.\n219:   selectivity is very low and the result outlives the source.\n222:**keeps the entire source buffer alive**. Filtering a million records down to three and holding\n271:guarantees, but that should be a decision rather than a side effect.\n295:Stream<T>  =  nuX. 1 + T*X    (greatest fixpoint: need not terminate, so possibly infinite)\n300:That single choice is the entire difference between an array and a stream.\n309:fn adults(db: Db) -> Stream<User> =\n312:fn limit(a: {count: Int, over: Stream<T>}) -> Stream<T> = ...\n318:the effect-layer operator. With only unary functions there is no argument list to disambiguate,\n499:The algebra is not new, either. [The regular-expressions-over-types idea](plans/questions.md#q4-can-the-type-express-ordering-over-heterogeneous-streams) already proposes `Alt<A,B>`, `Seq`,\n544:builds exactly a two-field record and silently ignores every other key in the source object,\n610:[The type-you-can-declare-but-cannot-build gap](research-log/a-type-you-can-declare-but-cannot-build.md) closes as a side effect of giving declarations a\n614:`input`, empty `[]`, and lambdas), and the free constructor is what gives that instance a name\n634:the same representation [the regular-expressions-over-types idea](plans/questions.md#q4-can-the-type-express-ordering-over-heterogeneous-streams) assigns each type (`Alt` for a\n652:mode. The decoder takes untyped input and can fail on every field. They cannot be the same\n666:## One combinator algebra for trees, strings, and streams\n681:Spiewak) is the same idea again, and it is also what [the regex-over-types entry](plans/questions.md#q4-can-the-type-express-ordering-over-heterogeneous-streams) is already\n688:stream needs an actual parsing engine underneath, because \"does `a*` match here\" can require\n693:[the batch-invariance law](#the-admissible-input-set-and-where-batching-comes-from), but for \"matches the same shape\" rather than \"commutes with\n759:A `Stream` can only be walked once, so combining several independent accumulations over one\n760:stream needs first-class support:\n763:fn stats(xs: Stream<Int>) -> {sum: Int, count: Int, max: Int} =\n","metadata":{"output":"18:**Effect layer** means multiplicity is a property of *evaluation*. An expression that yields\n19:three things is not a container; it produced three results. The name is borrowed from effect\n20:systems, where an effect describes *how* an expression evaluates rather than what it returns.\n28:jq's defining feature is that every expression produces a *stream* of 0..n values. The insight\n29:this design rests on is that a stream and a collection are the same information stored in two\n44:.[]       value  -> effect      # reflect\n45:[ ... ]   effect -> value       # reify\n48:Everything else stays in its layer. `select`, `..`, `,` and `//` live in the effect layer;\n64:If multiplicity in the effect layer is purely an evaluation-time phenomenon, with no stream\n65:*values* existing at all, then the two distinctions collapse into one. The effect layer is then\n68:**Chosen: the collapse, with a spelling.** Multiplicity in the effect layer is\n69:evaluation-level, and `Stream<T>` is its type -- the type of an expression that yields its\n70:entries as evaluation proceeds, not the type of a stream object. The cardinality table below\n72:of this document read as contradicting each other here. The alternative, streams as\n73:first-class values, would have meant three things to track (`Vec`, a `Stream` value, and\n74:effect multiplicity) and a held value of genuinely unknown extent. See\n75:[the decision](#decided-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed) for the full rules.\n83:Stream<T>    0..n, possibly infinite, indexing only by iteration\n86:`Vec` and `Stream` are not \"eager\" and \"lazy\". The honest difference is the **promise they\n87:make**. `Vec` guarantees indexing without iteration; `Stream` does not, and may be infinite.\n91:the foo[] operator is actually just applying another vector as a projection on a vector or a stream, so that would be\n94:The filter `.[3]` would be getting the third item of each value in the stream. If a 2D vector is projected by a vector of length one, then you'd get not single items, but a one dimensional vector.\n97:memory and rather than beocming streams since they have fixed cardinality, even if that si also only derived from their \"parent\" object. This means that even `.[]` only becomes a stream if `.` was already a stream, I guess. but when to materialize is another question: i guess that `.[] | select ...etc ...` would be allowed to materialiez teh resutl instead of making it a lens, even tho it could appear like a lens in the type system, that is if we can assume that no more references to `.` exist elsewhere.\n106:thing that turns a value into effect-layer multiplicity. Under projection-with-lenses, `.[]` on\n110:the effect layer would then arise only from genuinely unknown cardinality (I/O, `select`, `..`),\n124:effect-layer multiplicity at all. What does? Only things with genuinely unknown extent: I/O,\n130:Keep pulling and the effect layer shrinks to one thing: genuine streaming input whose length is\n133:than two, running one way, `[...]` collapsing a stream into a value.\n136:`Stream[3]` consumes three items and cannot yield a view. Different cost, different guarantee,\n154:**Third option: there is no value-to-effect shifter, because it would only destroy\n155:information.** Ask what converting a `Vec` into effect multiplicity actually buys. Effect-layer\n160:Under this reading, effect multiplicity is *born* from streaming sources whose length has not\n166:born effect, collapsed to a value. Writing a `Vec` to stdout iterates internally and needs no\n167:type-level stream. Avoiding an intermediate materialization is fusion, which is a compiler\n172:It answers [the streams question](plans/questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity). If effect multiplicity is only ever born from I/O and only ever collapses into\n173:values, there is no need for a `Stream<T>` *type* at all. `Stream` becomes an effect annotation\n182:first-class `Stream` value would be, so [the streams question](plans/questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity) and this proposal stand or fall together.\n202:bitmap            1 bit per SOURCE element.  Cheap to AND together.  Indexing needs rank.\n217:   proportional to survivors rather than to sources.\n219:   selectivity is very low and the result outlives the source.\n222:**keeps the entire source buffer alive**. Filtering a million records down to three and holding\n271:guarantees, but that should be a decision rather than a side effect.\n295:Stream<T>  =  nuX. 1 + T*X    (greatest fixpoint: need not terminate, so possibly infinite)\n300:That single choice is the entire difference between an array and a stream.\n309:fn adults(db: Db) -> Stream<User> =\n312:fn limit(a: {count: Int, over: Stream<T>}) -> Stream<T> = ...\n318:the effect-layer operator. With only unary functions there is no argument list to disambiguate,\n499:The algebra is not new, either. [The regular-expressions-over-types idea](plans/questions.md#q4-can-the-type-express-ordering-over-heterogeneous-streams) already proposes `Alt<A,B>`, `Seq`,\n544:builds exactly a two-field record and silently ignores every other key in the source object,\n610:[The type-you-can-declare-but-cannot-build gap](research-log/a-type-you-can-declare-but-cannot-build.md) closes as a side effect of giving declarations a\n614:`input`, empty `[]`, and lambdas), and the free constructor is what gives that instance a name\n634:the same representation [the regular-expressions-over-types idea](plans/questions.md#q4-can-the-type-express-ordering-over-heterogeneous-streams) assigns each type (`Alt` for a\n652:mode. The decoder takes untyped input and can fail on every field. They cannot be the same\n666:## One combinator algebra for trees, strings, and streams\n681:Spiewak) is the same idea again, and it is also what [the regex-over-types entry](plans/questions.md#q4-can-the-type-express-ordering-over-heterogeneous-streams) is already\n688:stream needs an actual parsing engine underneath, because \"does `a*` match here\" can require\n693:[the batch-invariance law](#the-admissible-input-set-and-where-batching-comes-from), but for \"matches the same shape\" rather than \"commutes with\n759:A `Stream` can only be walked once, so combining several independent accumulations over one\n760:stream needs first-class support:\n763:fn stats(xs: Stream<Int>) -> {sum: Int, count: Int, max: Int} =\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -rn -i \"stream\\|effect\\|input\\|output\\|sink\\|source\" draft.md | head -60","time":{"start":1788297085320,"end":1788297085325}},"id":"prt_05ecfff070010CDUqChqqDm12V","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","messageID":"msg_05ecfe8d50012HRmdMlleq72WO"}}
