{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297089915,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_9ed8f08047174370ae985496","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"ls tests/corpus/ | head -30; echo \"---\"; ls tests/corpus/ | wc -l"},"output":"adults.yaml\narith_add.yaml\narith_div_trunc.yaml\narith_mul.yaml\narith_neg.yaml\narith_precedence.yaml\narith_rem_sign.yaml\narith_sub.yaml\nbool_connectives.yaml\nbool_or_in_arm_body.yaml\nbool_or_splits_from_arms.yaml\nbool_precedence.yaml\nbool_result.yaml\nbool_short_circuit.yaml\ncall_without_parens.yaml\nchar_range_and_complement.yaml\nchars_unicode_honest.yaml\ncomparison_semantics.yaml\nconcat_chain.yaml\nconcat.yaml\ncond_branches_from_annotation.yaml\ncond_chain.yaml\ncond_false.yaml\ncond_in_map.yaml\ncond_true.yaml\ncontrol_char_escapes.yaml\ncsv_partial.yaml\ndiv_by_zero.yaml\ndsv_blank_line_is_an_empty_field.yaml\ndsv_rows.yaml\n---\n221\n","metadata":{"output":"adults.yaml\narith_add.yaml\narith_div_trunc.yaml\narith_mul.yaml\narith_neg.yaml\narith_precedence.yaml\narith_rem_sign.yaml\narith_sub.yaml\nbool_connectives.yaml\nbool_or_in_arm_body.yaml\nbool_or_splits_from_arms.yaml\nbool_precedence.yaml\nbool_result.yaml\nbool_short_circuit.yaml\ncall_without_parens.yaml\nchar_range_and_complement.yaml\nchars_unicode_honest.yaml\ncomparison_semantics.yaml\nconcat_chain.yaml\nconcat.yaml\ncond_branches_from_annotation.yaml\ncond_chain.yaml\ncond_false.yaml\ncond_in_map.yaml\ncond_true.yaml\ncontrol_char_escapes.yaml\ncsv_partial.yaml\ndiv_by_zero.yaml\ndsv_blank_line_is_an_empty_field.yaml\ndsv_rows.yaml\n---\n221\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"ls tests/corpus/ | head -30; echo \"---\"; ls tests/corpus/ | wc -l","time":{"start":1788297089911,"end":1788297089914}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me understand the overall structure. Let me look at draft.md more thoroughly, particularly the stream/effect layer sections, and understand how inputs are modeled. Let me also look at how the toylang programs interact with input/output. Let me check the tests/corpus to see real toylang programs with I/O.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ed0136d0018f3Q4AeJV0P8yR","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","messageID":"msg_05ed001a7001jQ8U7RmpkgB9Ia"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297089918,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_b4956d1fdea54863a53626fc","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-http-query-sugar-research/draft.md","limit":140},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-http-query-sugar-research/draft.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # toylang design draft\n2: \n3: **Status: exploratory. Everything here is provisional.** This is a thinking document, not a\n4: specification. Syntax is illustrative; several core decisions are still open (see the end).\n5: \n6: ## The core idea: two layers\n7: \n8: ### Vocabulary\n9: \n10: Four words are used precisely throughout this document.\n11: \n12: **Cardinality** is how many values something produces. Not *which* values, only how many:\n13: exactly one, zero-or-one, zero-or-more.\n14: \n15: **Value layer** means multiplicity is stored *in a value*. An array of three things is one\n16: value that happens to contain three.\n17: \n18: **Effect layer** means multiplicity is a property of *evaluation*. An expression that yields\n19: three things is not a container; it produced three results. The name is borrowed from effect\n20: systems, where an effect describes *how* an expression evaluates rather than what it returns.\n21: \n22: **Reify and reflect** are the two directions of crossing between layers. Reify means \"make it\n23: a thing\": collect evaluation multiplicity into a value. Reflect is the opposite, taking a\n24: value's contents and turning them back into evaluation multiplicity.\n25: \n26: ### The idea\n27: \n28: jq's defining feature is that every expression produces a *stream* of 0..n values. The insight\n29: this design rests on is that a stream and a collection are the same information stored in two\n30: different places:\n31: \n32: ```\n33: [1,2,3] | map(select(. > 1))     # -> [2,3]     multiplicity became array length\n34: [1,2,3] | .[] | select(. > 1)    # -> 2, 3      multiplicity stayed in the evaluation\n35: ```\n36: \n37: Same computation, same values. The only difference is *where the multiplicity lives*.\n38: \n39: So there are two layers, and **exactly two operators cross between them**. These are the only\n40: layer shifters in the language. Nothing else moves between layers, and neither one is ever\n41: inserted implicitly:\n42: \n43: ```\n44: .[]       value  -> effect      # reflect\n45: [ ... ]   effect -> value       # reify\n46: ```\n47: \n48: Everything else stays in its layer. `select`, `..`, `,` and `//` live in the effect layer;\n49: `length`, `+` and `sort` live in the value layer; `|` composes within a layer.\n50: \n51: This is why `map` and `select` do **not** need to be different kinds of thing. `map(f)` is\n52: sugar for `[ .[] | f ]`, which is reflect, apply, reify. It is cardinality-polymorphic in its\n53: argument and cardinality-collapsing in its result, which is exactly why `map(select(p))`\n54: typechecks and returns a plain array.\n55: \n56: ### Relationship to \"index vs. iterate\"\n57: \n58: These are related but not identical distinctions, and how they relate is currently an open\n59: question (see open question 1).\n60: \n61: Layer is about where multiplicity is *stored*. Index versus iterate is about what access a type\n62: *promises*: constant-time positional access, or sequential walking only.\n63: \n64: If multiplicity in the effect layer is purely an evaluation-time phenomenon, with no stream\n65: *values* existing at all, then the two distinctions collapse into one. The effect layer is then\n66: the only iterate-only thing, and `Vec` is the only multiplicity-bearing value.\n67: \n68: **Chosen: the collapse, with a spelling.** Multiplicity in the effect layer is\n69: evaluation-level, and `Stream<T>` is its type -- the type of an expression that yields its\n70: entries as evaluation proceeds, not the type of a stream object. The cardinality table below\n71: and this section were describing the same thing from two sides, which is why earlier versions\n72: of this document read as contradicting each other here. The alternative, streams as\n73: first-class values, would have meant three things to track (`Vec`, a `Stream` value, and\n74: effect multiplicity) and a held value of genuinely unknown extent. See\n75: [the decision](#decided-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed) for the full rules.\n76: \n77: ## Cardinality is part of the type\n78: \n79: ```\n80: T            exactly one\n81: Opt<T>       0..1\n82: Vec<T>       0..n, finite, indexing without iteration\n83: Stream<T>    0..n, possibly infinite, indexing only by iteration\n84: ```\n85: \n86: `Vec` and `Stream` are not \"eager\" and \"lazy\". The honest difference is the **promise they\n87: make**. `Vec` guarantees indexing without iteration; `Stream` does not, and may be infinite.\n88: That asymmetry is load-bearing, as `fold` below shows.\n89: \n90: TODO: explore idea that Vectors are multidimensional, so their cardiniality is expressed per-dimension;\n91: the foo[] operator is actually just applying another vector as a projection on a vector or a stream, so that would be\n92: a \"project\" trait. (I guess we could have a convention that traits are methods with __ as prefix and suffix liek in python\n93: so basically, if you havea  vector \"foo\", that is one dimensional, then foo[3] would be just getting the third item. the projectsion itslef is actually the vector `[3]`.\n94: The filter `.[3]` would be getting the third item of each value in the stream. If a 2D vector is projected by a vector of length one, then you'd get not single items, but a one dimensional vector.\n95: there could also be some elegant way of specifying when you want to project not along the first dimension, but perhaps that would be an operator like `.{1: 3}` which woudl mean that get the subvector\n96: where there is one less dimension and only the items where the orignal dimension 1's coordinate is \"3\" are returned. but I guess that these projections coudl become lenses, rahter than hard copies in\n97: memory and rather than beocming streams since they have fixed cardinality, even if that si also only derived from their \"parent\" object. This means that even `.[]` only becomes a stream if `.` was already a stream, I guess. but when to materialize is another question: i guess that `.[] | select ...etc ...` would be allowed to materialiez teh resutl instead of making it a lens, even tho it could appear like a lens in the type system, that is if we can assume that no more references to `.` exist elsewhere.\n98: \n99: RESPONSE (agent). Four things this does, one of which breaks something above.\n100: \n101: It answers \"index vs. iterate\" by dissolving it. If `[]` is projection by a vector, then `foo[3]`\n102: and `foo[]` are one operator with different projections, `[3]` and \"all indices\". That is a\n103: better answer than treating them as two access modes, and it is what NumPy and APL do.\n104: \n105: It contradicts the two-layer section. That section says `.[]` is *the* reflect operator, the\n106: thing that turns a value into effect-layer multiplicity. Under projection-with-lenses, `.[]` on\n107: a `Vec` yields a `Vec` view with known cardinality, which never leaves the value layer. So\n108: either `.[]` stops being a layer shifter, or projection is a different operator that happens to\n109: share syntax. Worth noting the version where `.[]` stops shifting layers is arguably *better*:\n110: the effect layer would then arise only from genuinely unknown cardinality (I/O, `select`, `..`),\n111: and everything with statically known extent stays a value, which is also exactly what\n112: vectorizes. But the claim \"exactly two layer shifters\" does not survive it unchanged.\n113: \n114: --> but is this a true contradiction, or is this somethign that actually a trait that is implemented\n115: over everythign that has the index trait, and separately over anythign that has the iteration trait?\n116: \n117: but perhaps the implementations on both can actuallyb e slightly different, as there may be some edge cases that do not work in both\n118: \n119: RESPONSE (agent). The trait framing works, and it resolves more than it claims. Not \"this is\n120: not a contradiction\" but \"`.[]` was never the layer shifter in the first place.\"\n121: \n122: Follow it through. If `__project__` has an impl for indexable things and another for iterable\n123: ones, then `Vec[]` returns a `Vec` view and stays in the value layer. So `.[]` does not create\n124: effect-layer multiplicity at all. What does? Only things with genuinely unknown extent: I/O,\n125: `..`, and `select`.\n126: \n127: Except that under the columnar model `select` does not either. A predicate over a batch is a\n128: mask, and masking a `Vec` gives a `Vec`. So it also stays in the value layer.\n129: \n130: Keep pulling and the effect layer shrinks to one thing: genuine streaming input whose length is\n131: unknown because it has not arrived yet. That is a much smaller and much more defensible notion\n132: than \"anything with more than one value\", and it would mean there is one layer shifter rather\n133: than two, running one way, `[...]` collapsing a stream into a value.\n134: \n135: The two impls differing is fine and expected. `Vec[3]` is constant time and yields a view;\n136: `Stream[3]` consumes three items and cannot yield a view. Different cost, different guarantee,\n137: same trait. Symmetry is satisfied because the type says which one you got.\n138: \n139: ### PROPOSAL: the layer shift only runs one way\n140: \n\n(Showing lines 1-140 of 2470. Use offset=141 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# toylang design draft\n\n**Status: exploratory. Everything here is provisional.** This is a thinking document, not a\nspecification. Syntax is illustrative; several core decisions are still open (see the end).\n\n## The core idea: two layers\n\n### Vocabulary\n\nFour words are used precisely throughout this document.\n\n**Cardinality** is how many values something produces. Not *which* values, only how many:\nexactly one, zero-or-one, zero-or-more.\n\n**Value layer** means multiplicity is stored *in a value*. An array of three things is one\nvalue that happens to contain three.\n\n**Effect layer** means multiplicity is a property of *evaluation*. An expression that yields\nthree things is not a container; it produced three results. The name is borrowed from effect\nsystems, where an effect describes *how* an expression evaluates rather than what it returns.","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-http-query-sugar-research/draft.md","text":"# toylang design draft\n\n**Status: exploratory. Everything here is provisional.** This is a thinking document, not a\nspecification. Syntax is illustrative; several core decisions are still open (see the end).\n\n## The core idea: two layers\n\n### Vocabulary\n\nFour words are used precisely throughout this document.\n\n**Cardinality** is how many values something produces. Not *which* values, only how many:\nexactly one, zero-or-one, zero-or-more.\n\n**Value layer** means multiplicity is stored *in a value*. An array of three things is one\nvalue that happens to contain three.\n\n**Effect layer** means multiplicity is a property of *evaluation*. An expression that yields\nthree things is not a container; it produced three results. The name is borrowed from effect\nsystems, where an effect describes *how* an expression evaluates rather than what it returns.\n\n**Reify and reflect** are the two directions of crossing between layers. Reify means \"make it\na thing\": collect evaluation multiplicity into a value. Reflect is the opposite, taking a\nvalue's contents and turning them back into evaluation multiplicity.\n\n### The idea\n\njq's defining feature is that every expression produces a *stream* of 0..n values. The insight\nthis design rests on is that a stream and a collection are the same information stored in two\ndifferent places:\n\n```\n[1,2,3] | map(select(. > 1))     # -> [2,3]     multiplicity became array length\n[1,2,3] | .[] | select(. > 1)    # -> 2, 3      multiplicity stayed in the evaluation\n```\n\nSame computation, same values. The only difference is *where the multiplicity lives*.\n\nSo there are two layers, and **exactly two operators cross between them**. These are the only\nlayer shifters in the language. Nothing else moves between layers, and neither one is ever\ninserted implicitly:\n\n```\n.[]       value  -> effect      # reflect\n[ ... ]   effect -> value       # reify\n```\n\nEverything else stays in its layer. `select`, `..`, `,` and `//` live in the effect layer;\n`length`, `+` and `sort` live in the value layer; `|` composes within a layer.\n\nThis is why `map` and `select` do **not** need to be different kinds of thing. `map(f)` is\nsugar for `[ .[] | f ]`, which is reflect, apply, reify. It is cardinality-polymorphic in its\nargument and cardinality-collapsing in its result, which is exactly why `map(select(p))`\ntypechecks and returns a plain array.\n\n### Relationship to \"index vs. iterate\"\n\nThese are related but not identical distinctions, and how they relate is currently an open\nquestion (see open question 1).\n\nLayer is about where multiplicity is *stored*. Index versus iterate is about what access a type\n*promises*: constant-time positional access, or sequential walking only.\n\nIf multiplicity in the effect layer is purely an evaluation-time phenomenon, with no stream\n*values* existing at all, then the two distinctions collapse into one. The effect layer is then\nthe only iterate-only thing, and `Vec` is the only multiplicity-bearing value.\n\n**Chosen: the collapse, with a spelling.** Multiplicity in the effect layer is\nevaluation-level, and `Stream<T>` is its type -- the type of an expression that yields its\nentries as evaluation proceeds, not the type of a stream object. The cardinality table below\nand this section were describing the same thing from two sides, which is why earlier versions\nof this document read as contradicting each other here. The alternative, streams as\nfirst-class values, would have meant three things to track (`Vec`, a `Stream` value, and\neffect multiplicity) and a held value of genuinely unknown extent. See\n[the decision](#decided-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed) for the full rules.\n\n## Cardinality is part of the type\n\n```\nT            exactly one\nOpt<T>       0..1\nVec<T>       0..n, finite, indexing without iteration\nStream<T>    0..n, possibly infinite, indexing only by iteration\n```\n\n`Vec` and `Stream` are not \"eager\" and \"lazy\". The honest difference is the **promise they\nmake**. `Vec` guarantees indexing without iteration; `Stream` does not, and may be infinite.\nThat asymmetry is load-bearing, as `fold` below shows.\n\nTODO: explore idea that Vectors are multidimensional, so their cardiniality is expressed per-dimension;\nthe foo[] operator is actually just applying another vector as a projection on a vector or a stream, so that would be\na \"project\" trait. (I guess we could have a convention that traits are methods with __ as prefix and suffix liek in python\nso basically, if you havea  vector \"foo\", that is one dimensional, then foo[3] would be just getting the third item. the projectsion itslef is actually the vector `[3]`.\nThe filter `.[3]` would be getting the third item of each value in the stream. If a 2D vector is projected by a vector of length one, then you'd get not single items, but a one dimensional vector.\nthere could also be some elegant way of specifying when you want to project not along the first dimension, but perhaps that would be an operator like `.{1: 3}` which woudl mean that get the subvector\nwhere there is one less dimension and only the items where the orignal dimension 1's coordinate is \"3\" are returned. but I guess that these projections coudl become lenses, rahter than hard copies in\nmemory and rather than beocming streams since they have fixed cardinality, even if that si also only derived from their \"parent\" object. This means that even `.[]` only becomes a stream if `.` was already a stream, I guess. but when to materialize is another question: i guess that `.[] | select ...etc ...` would be allowed to materialiez teh resutl instead of making it a lens, even tho it could appear like a lens in the type system, that is if we can assume that no more references to `.` exist elsewhere.\n\nRESPONSE (agent). Four things this does, one of which breaks something above.\n\nIt answers \"index vs. iterate\" by dissolving it. If `[]` is projection by a vector, then `foo[3]`\nand `foo[]` are one operator with different projections, `[3]` and \"all indices\". That is a\nbetter answer than treating them as two access modes, and it is what NumPy and APL do.\n\nIt contradicts the two-layer section. That section says `.[]` is *the* reflect operator, the\nthing that turns a value into effect-layer multiplicity. Under projection-with-lenses, `.[]` on\na `Vec` yields a `Vec` view with known cardinality, which never leaves the value layer. So\neither `.[]` stops being a layer shifter, or projection is a different operator that happens to\nshare syntax. Worth noting the version where `.[]` stops shifting layers is arguably *better*:\nthe effect layer would then arise only from genuinely unknown cardinality (I/O, `select`, `..`),\nand everything with statically known extent stays a value, which is also exactly what\nvectorizes. But the claim \"exactly two layer shifters\" does not survive it unchanged.\n\n--> but is this a true contradiction, or is this somethign that actually a trait that is implemented\nover everythign that has the index trait, and separately over anythign that has the iteration trait?\n\nbut perhaps the implementations on both can actuallyb e slightly different, as there may be some edge cases that do not work in both\n\nRESPONSE (agent). The trait framing works, and it resolves more than it claims. Not \"this is\nnot a contradiction\" but \"`.[]` was never the layer shifter in the first place.\"\n\nFollow it through. If `__project__` has an impl for indexable things and another for iterable\nones, then `Vec[]` returns a `Vec` view and stays in the value layer. So `.[]` does not create\neffect-layer multiplicity at all. What does? Only things with genuinely unknown extent: I/O,\n`..`, and `select`.\n\nExcept that under the columnar model `select` does not either. A predicate over a batch is a\nmask, and masking a `Vec` gives a `Vec`. So it also stays in the value layer.\n\nKeep pulling and the effect layer shrinks to one thing: genuine streaming input whose length is\nunknown because it has not arrived yet. That is a much smaller and much more defensible notion\nthan \"anything with more than one value\", and it would mean there is one layer shifter rather\nthan two, running one way, `[...]` collapsing a stream into a value.\n\nThe two impls differing is fine and expected. `Vec[3]` is constant time and yields a view;\n`Stream[3]` consumes three items and cannot yield a view. Different cost, different guarantee,\nsame trait. Symmetry is satisfied because the type says which one you got.\n\n### PROPOSAL: the layer shift only runs one way\n","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":140,"totalLines":2470,"truncated":true}},"title":"draft.md","time":{"start":1788297089907,"end":1788297089917}},"id":"prt_05ed013710013z2yRVgEqKkRKd","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","messageID":"msg_05ed001a7001jQ8U7RmpkgB9Ia"}}
