{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297097653,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_293bdb4975f34e76a717d29b","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -n \"^#\\|^##\\|^###\" draft.md | head -80"},"output":"1:# toylang design draft\n6:## The core idea: two layers\n8:### Vocabulary\n26:### The idea\n56:### Relationship to \"index vs. iterate\"\n77:## Cardinality is part of the type\n139:### PROPOSAL: the layer shift only runs one way\n184:### Does a value-layer `select` copy?\n304:## Functions are unary\n321:### Annotation rule: named functions declare, lambdas never do\n337:#           ^^^^^^^^^^ checked against `A -> B`\n338:#                      A = User is known from `over`, so `u : User` needs no annotation\n339:#                      B = Str is learned from the body\n367:#              ^ from map's signature   ^ from filter's signature\n374:#                   ^^^^^^^^^^^^ this is what types the lambda\n400:## Field access is a lens\n431:## Pattern matching is decoding\n585:#### The constructor should be free, and that reverses a dependency claimed above\n666:## One combinator algebra for trees, strings, and streams\n722:## Query is search\n757:## Single-pass composition\n781:### `reduce` and `fold` are different operations\n803:## Backends, vectorization, and the offload boundary\n805:### Cardinality is the kernel-admissibility predicate\n825:#### This contradicts an earlier claim, and the earlier claim was wrong\n846:#### But the mapping needs a precision about granularity\n858:### The admissible input set, and where batching comes from\n922:### The primitive set cannot be fold and recursion\n1003:### The same predicate governs CPU vectorization\n1034:### Backend choice\n1072:### A dense tensor type\n1134:## Strings are where platform independence actually costs something\n1158:## Mutation\n1171:### UNDECIDED: what to call the record-forming update\n1221:## Why cardinality-in-the-type is the safety mechanism\n1247:## PROPOSAL: every dimension gets a spec\n1253:### Where it came from\n1263:### The proposal\n1279:### What follows without further stipulation\n1302:### Consequences to accept\n1318:### Settled\n1335:### Unwrapping\n1351:### Still open, and none of it blocks building this\n1357:## DECIDED: Int is 32 bits and wraps\n1364:### The decision\n1371:### Why not wider\n1381:### Why wrapping rather than trapping\n1393:### What this costs, and what pays it back later\n1424:### The operators, and the one way they fail\n1453:### The literal was where the rule leaked, and a fifth backend found it\n1478:### Six backends, and why there are six\n1510:### Output needs no side effect\n1547:## What the prototype showed\n1560:### Stream lowering does not block a backend that does not stream\n1572:### The one-way layer shift held, and it has a price\n1588:### Vectorizability fell out of the layout without being declared\n1599:### Masking now has an implementation to argue with\n1609:### The native backend is built\n1616:### Three string representations now disagree in a specific place\n1623:### Two claims above are contradicted by what got built\n1650:## DECIDED: records can be built, and a record is how several arguments travel\n1656:### The form\n1667:### Why a record and not a map\n1684:### One meaning, and `map` is the only thing that crosses a dimension\n1694:### `{}` is legal where `[]` is not\n1712:### Punning is out\n1723:### Functions stay unary, and a record is how several arguments travel\n1742:### Argument parens are optional when the argument is a record literal\n1766:### What it costs the native backend\n1773:## DECIDED: a minimal cut of streaming input, pull-based, one new keyword\n1782:### Pull, not push\n1802:### `lines`, `collect`, and nothing else exposed\n1834:### The line rule, checked rather than assumed\n1851:### What \"each backend reads for itself\" actually meant to build\n1882:### What this does not attempt\n1896:## DECIDED: one stdin source is the destination\n1908:## DECIDED: `inputs`, eager, not an answer to Q1 either\n1958:## DECIDED: `jsonlines`, and the jq tutorial reproduces in full\n1995:## DECIDED: `f x` reads as `f(x)`, but only where an expression begins fresh\n2059:## DECIDED: a rudimentary module system, one prelude file and `pub`\n","metadata":{"output":"1:# toylang design draft\n6:## The core idea: two layers\n8:### Vocabulary\n26:### The idea\n56:### Relationship to \"index vs. iterate\"\n77:## Cardinality is part of the type\n139:### PROPOSAL: the layer shift only runs one way\n184:### Does a value-layer `select` copy?\n304:## Functions are unary\n321:### Annotation rule: named functions declare, lambdas never do\n337:#           ^^^^^^^^^^ checked against `A -> B`\n338:#                      A = User is known from `over`, so `u : User` needs no annotation\n339:#                      B = Str is learned from the body\n367:#              ^ from map's signature   ^ from filter's signature\n374:#                   ^^^^^^^^^^^^ this is what types the lambda\n400:## Field access is a lens\n431:## Pattern matching is decoding\n585:#### The constructor should be free, and that reverses a dependency claimed above\n666:## One combinator algebra for trees, strings, and streams\n722:## Query is search\n757:## Single-pass composition\n781:### `reduce` and `fold` are different operations\n803:## Backends, vectorization, and the offload boundary\n805:### Cardinality is the kernel-admissibility predicate\n825:#### This contradicts an earlier claim, and the earlier claim was wrong\n846:#### But the mapping needs a precision about granularity\n858:### The admissible input set, and where batching comes from\n922:### The primitive set cannot be fold and recursion\n1003:### The same predicate governs CPU vectorization\n1034:### Backend choice\n1072:### A dense tensor type\n1134:## Strings are where platform independence actually costs something\n1158:## Mutation\n1171:### UNDECIDED: what to call the record-forming update\n1221:## Why cardinality-in-the-type is the safety mechanism\n1247:## PROPOSAL: every dimension gets a spec\n1253:### Where it came from\n1263:### The proposal\n1279:### What follows without further stipulation\n1302:### Consequences to accept\n1318:### Settled\n1335:### Unwrapping\n1351:### Still open, and none of it blocks building this\n1357:## DECIDED: Int is 32 bits and wraps\n1364:### The decision\n1371:### Why not wider\n1381:### Why wrapping rather than trapping\n1393:### What this costs, and what pays it back later\n1424:### The operators, and the one way they fail\n1453:### The literal was where the rule leaked, and a fifth backend found it\n1478:### Six backends, and why there are six\n1510:### Output needs no side effect\n1547:## What the prototype showed\n1560:### Stream lowering does not block a backend that does not stream\n1572:### The one-way layer shift held, and it has a price\n1588:### Vectorizability fell out of the layout without being declared\n1599:### Masking now has an implementation to argue with\n1609:### The native backend is built\n1616:### Three string representations now disagree in a specific place\n1623:### Two claims above are contradicted by what got built\n1650:## DECIDED: records can be built, and a record is how several arguments travel\n1656:### The form\n1667:### Why a record and not a map\n1684:### One meaning, and `map` is the only thing that crosses a dimension\n1694:### `{}` is legal where `[]` is not\n1712:### Punning is out\n1723:### Functions stay unary, and a record is how several arguments travel\n1742:### Argument parens are optional when the argument is a record literal\n1766:### What it costs the native backend\n1773:## DECIDED: a minimal cut of streaming input, pull-based, one new keyword\n1782:### Pull, not push\n1802:### `lines`, `collect`, and nothing else exposed\n1834:### The line rule, checked rather than assumed\n1851:### What \"each backend reads for itself\" actually meant to build\n1882:### What this does not attempt\n1896:## DECIDED: one stdin source is the destination\n1908:## DECIDED: `inputs`, eager, not an answer to Q1 either\n1958:## DECIDED: `jsonlines`, and the jq tutorial reproduces in full\n1995:## DECIDED: `f x` reads as `f(x)`, but only where an expression begins fresh\n2059:## DECIDED: a rudimentary module system, one prelude file and `pub`\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -n \"^#\\|^##\\|^###\" draft.md | head -80","time":{"start":1788297097648,"end":1788297097649}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the source vocabulary: `input` (single JSON value from stdin), `inputs` (stream of JSON values one per line), `lines` (raw lines from stdin), `dsv(\";\")` (parameterized DSV source). The sink side: the program's output is printed (likely as JSON via stdout).\n\nLet me look at draft.md's stream/effect layer sections more. And let me check the docs directory for the reference pages that describe the language. Let me search draft.md for \"input\" and \"source\" sections, and also check what the language says about external effects / I/O.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ed03195001dxMqQO9C3piDVp","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","messageID":"msg_05ed0253d001OI6foPwrIdHYYs"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297097653,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_868228fd933c40499588f304","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -n -i \"input\\|stdin\\|source\\|stdout\\|print\\|output\\|http\\|network\\|curl\" draft.md | head -50"},"output":"130:Keep pulling and the effect layer shrinks to one thing: genuine streaming input whose length is\n160:Under this reading, effect multiplicity is *born* from streaming sources whose length has not\n165:| select(p))` is `Vec -> Vec -> Opt`, still value layer. `stdin.lines | select(p) | [...]` is\n166:born effect, collapsed to a value. Writing a `Vec` to stdout iterates internally and needs no\n202:bitmap            1 bit per SOURCE element.  Cheap to AND together.  Indexing needs rank.\n217:   proportional to survivors rather than to sources.\n219:   selectivity is very low and the result outlives the source.\n222:**keeps the entire source buffer alive**. Filtering a million records down to three and holding\n395:stdin.lines | parse_json? | select(.level == \"ERROR\") | .service\n544:builds exactly a two-field record and silently ignores every other key in the source object,\n608:generated by the declaration *is* object construction, so there is no separate curly-brace\n614:`input`, empty `[]`, and lambdas), and the free constructor is what gives that instance a name\n652:mode. The decoder takes untyped input and can fail on every field. They cannot be the same\n693:[the batch-invariance law](#the-admissible-input-set-and-where-batching-comes-from), but for \"matches the same shape\" rather than \"commutes with\n792:The distinction then lives in the source text rather than in a pragma. A reader seeing `fold`\n812:One<T>       exactly one output per input   ->  elementwise map kernel\n823:path expressions, update assignment, `error`, and `input`.\n853:Neither are `group_by` or a join. These are the blocking operators, they need the whole input\n858:### The admissible input set, and where batching comes from\n874:than a limitation: **reification is where allocation becomes visible in the source**, so the one\n878:language, invisibly. **The input reader batches**, and its batching scheme appears in the type:\n881:stdin           Stream<Vec<T>>      batched by the reader, the batching is in the type\n882:your own source Vec<T>              you choose: one big vector, or batch it yourself\n978:size and a program can tell what it picked, results vary by input source and the semantics stop\n992:but not by the same path: the second needs a reduction before the output buffer can be sized,\n1019:| aliasing, the most common cause | immutable inputs and a distinct output buffer, so `noalias` is emitable and the runtime overlap check disappears |\n1049:wasm output          wasm32 target                   none; Cranelift consumes wasm\n1056:And if the browser story ever becomes WebAssembly rather than emitted JavaScript source, LLVM\n1082:input, nulls, or nested strings, at the constructor rather than three stages later. It\n1098:TODO (user): stdin and stdout should not require solving generic effect types, because they are\n1099:always string streams and so have no choice of element type. At worst the end of input shows up\n1113:**The pragmatic move is right.** stdin and stdout are byte streams with no element type to\n1130:survive `Stream` later becoming an annotation rather than a type. The safe version keeps stdin\n1131:and stdout as opaque handles that only a small set of operations touch, so that the operations\n1181:property-test input enumeration for free. The problem is purely that `=` *looks* like mutation\n1223:jq's multi-output semantics produce hazards that are all the same shape, namely the right\n1313:| `.[]` at top level | many outputs | one value; there is no stream |\n1342:The type is what decides whether output is raw, so unwrapping changes it. `[\"ada\",\"bo\"][0]` is\n1343:`Opt<Str>` and prints `\"ada\"`; `[\"ada\",\"bo\"][0]!` is `Str` and prints `ada`.\n1353:Whether records are open or closed, which the input rule already prejudges by ignoring\n1396:typecheck and then be rejected by the input validator on real data. That is a loud failure with a\n1419:`i64(x)`, and `input` refuses the type until its wire codec is designed\n1455:An `Int` literal wider than 32 bits used to be accepted, and all four backends printed it back\n1503:what `json.loads` already returns, so reading input is the parse and nothing else, where Go\n1510:### Output needs no side effect\n1512:A top-level `Str` already prints raw, so a string containing newlines is line-oriented output.\n1513:What was missing was not a way to print but a way to build that string, which is `unlines`, a\n1527:Adding a `print` instead would have been the language's first side effect, and a much larger step\n1528:than it looks. There is no answer to what `map(print(...))` returns in an expression language;\n1533:by the splitting direction that `stdin.lines` needs. The general `join(over, with)` cannot be\n","metadata":{"output":"130:Keep pulling and the effect layer shrinks to one thing: genuine streaming input whose length is\n160:Under this reading, effect multiplicity is *born* from streaming sources whose length has not\n165:| select(p))` is `Vec -> Vec -> Opt`, still value layer. `stdin.lines | select(p) | [...]` is\n166:born effect, collapsed to a value. Writing a `Vec` to stdout iterates internally and needs no\n202:bitmap            1 bit per SOURCE element.  Cheap to AND together.  Indexing needs rank.\n217:   proportional to survivors rather than to sources.\n219:   selectivity is very low and the result outlives the source.\n222:**keeps the entire source buffer alive**. Filtering a million records down to three and holding\n395:stdin.lines | parse_json? | select(.level == \"ERROR\") | .service\n544:builds exactly a two-field record and silently ignores every other key in the source object,\n608:generated by the declaration *is* object construction, so there is no separate curly-brace\n614:`input`, empty `[]`, and lambdas), and the free constructor is what gives that instance a name\n652:mode. The decoder takes untyped input and can fail on every field. They cannot be the same\n693:[the batch-invariance law](#the-admissible-input-set-and-where-batching-comes-from), but for \"matches the same shape\" rather than \"commutes with\n792:The distinction then lives in the source text rather than in a pragma. A reader seeing `fold`\n812:One<T>       exactly one output per input   ->  elementwise map kernel\n823:path expressions, update assignment, `error`, and `input`.\n853:Neither are `group_by` or a join. These are the blocking operators, they need the whole input\n858:### The admissible input set, and where batching comes from\n874:than a limitation: **reification is where allocation becomes visible in the source**, so the one\n878:language, invisibly. **The input reader batches**, and its batching scheme appears in the type:\n881:stdin           Stream<Vec<T>>      batched by the reader, the batching is in the type\n882:your own source Vec<T>              you choose: one big vector, or batch it yourself\n978:size and a program can tell what it picked, results vary by input source and the semantics stop\n992:but not by the same path: the second needs a reduction before the output buffer can be sized,\n1019:| aliasing, the most common cause | immutable inputs and a distinct output buffer, so `noalias` is emitable and the runtime overlap check disappears |\n1049:wasm output          wasm32 target                   none; Cranelift consumes wasm\n1056:And if the browser story ever becomes WebAssembly rather than emitted JavaScript source, LLVM\n1082:input, nulls, or nested strings, at the constructor rather than three stages later. It\n1098:TODO (user): stdin and stdout should not require solving generic effect types, because they are\n1099:always string streams and so have no choice of element type. At worst the end of input shows up\n1113:**The pragmatic move is right.** stdin and stdout are byte streams with no element type to\n1130:survive `Stream` later becoming an annotation rather than a type. The safe version keeps stdin\n1131:and stdout as opaque handles that only a small set of operations touch, so that the operations\n1181:property-test input enumeration for free. The problem is purely that `=` *looks* like mutation\n1223:jq's multi-output semantics produce hazards that are all the same shape, namely the right\n1313:| `.[]` at top level | many outputs | one value; there is no stream |\n1342:The type is what decides whether output is raw, so unwrapping changes it. `[\"ada\",\"bo\"][0]` is\n1343:`Opt<Str>` and prints `\"ada\"`; `[\"ada\",\"bo\"][0]!` is `Str` and prints `ada`.\n1353:Whether records are open or closed, which the input rule already prejudges by ignoring\n1396:typecheck and then be rejected by the input validator on real data. That is a loud failure with a\n1419:`i64(x)`, and `input` refuses the type until its wire codec is designed\n1455:An `Int` literal wider than 32 bits used to be accepted, and all four backends printed it back\n1503:what `json.loads` already returns, so reading input is the parse and nothing else, where Go\n1510:### Output needs no side effect\n1512:A top-level `Str` already prints raw, so a string containing newlines is line-oriented output.\n1513:What was missing was not a way to print but a way to build that string, which is `unlines`, a\n1527:Adding a `print` instead would have been the language's first side effect, and a much larger step\n1528:than it looks. There is no answer to what `map(print(...))` returns in an expression language;\n1533:by the splitting direction that `stdin.lines` needs. The general `join(over, with)` cannot be\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -n -i \"input\\|stdin\\|source\\|stdout\\|print\\|output\\|http\\|network\\|curl\" draft.md | head -50","time":{"start":1788297097650,"end":1788297097652}},"id":"prt_05ed031a9001omHTc0dBKUSbqK","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","messageID":"msg_05ed0253d001OI6foPwrIdHYYs"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788297097661,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"id":"prt_05ed031bb001ZY2s4VNkwf1R7t","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"1da03e262f4e207599f9c8f9109adaed8aee925b","messageID":"msg_05ed0253d001OI6foPwrIdHYYs","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":33157,"input":906,"output":113,"reasoning":138,"cache":{"write":0,"read":32000}},"cost":0.00061607}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788297101564,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"id":"prt_05ed040f2001RrumNyXkhH4kfF","messageID":"msg_05ed031cb001RN7IcI4Vtuce9M","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","snapshot":"1da03e262f4e207599f9c8f9109adaed8aee925b","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788297102598,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"id":"prt_05ed042be001BI35PxFcXxj74Q","messageID":"msg_05ed031cb001RN7IcI4Vtuce9M","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","type":"text","text":"\n\n","time":{"start":1788297102014,"end":1788297102595}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297102611,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_5de4ae5ea5fd4bfcab41e2b8","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-http-query-sugar-research/draft.md","limit":60,"offset":1505},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-http-query-sugar-research/draft.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1505: \n1506: It found no defect. See\n1507: [a backend that finds nothing is evidence only if it is different](research-log/a-backend-that-finds-nothing-is-evidence-only-if-it-is-different.md)\n1508: for what that is and is not worth.\n1509: \n1510: ### Output needs no side effect\n1511: \n1512: A top-level `Str` already prints raw, so a string containing newlines is line-oriented output.\n1513: What was missing was not a way to print but a way to build that string, which is `unlines`, a\n1514: pure conversion from `Vec<Str>`. FizzBuzz to a hundred is then an ordinary program:\n1515: \n1516: ```\n1517: unlines(\n1518:     range(100) | map(. + 1) | map(\n1519:         . % 15 == 0 -> \"FizzBuzz\" or\n1520:         . % 3 == 0  -> \"Fizz\"     or\n1521:         . % 5 == 0  -> \"Buzz\"     or\n1522:         str(.)\n1523:     )\n1524: )\n1525: ```\n1526: \n1527: Adding a `print` instead would have been the language's first side effect, and a much larger step\n1528: than it looks. There is no answer to what `map(print(...))` returns in an expression language;\n1529: ordering would become observable, which is exactly what stops a map being reordered or\n1530: vectorised; and the effect would not be in the type, which is what principle 2 exists to prevent.\n1531: \n1532: `unlines` is named for Haskell's rather than being called `lines`, because `lines` is spoken for\n1533: by the splitting direction that `stdin.lines` needs. The general `join(over, with)` cannot be\n1534: written at all: functions are unary, so a second argument means passing a record, and there is no\n1535: record literal to build one with. That is\n1536: [a type you can declare but cannot build](research-log/a-type-you-can-declare-but-cannot-build.md)\n1537: biting somewhere a user would actually reach.\n1538: \n1539: `range(n)` is zero-based, matching jq, Python, and this language's own indices, which is why the\n1540: example shifts with `map(. + 1)` rather than starting at one.\n1541: \n1542: **A guard is exactly one `Bool`**, which is the claim\n1543: [the safety section](#why-cardinality-in-the-type-is-the-safety-mechanism) makes: a match arm's\n1544: left side must be a Bool, where jq would run both branches of its `if` and hand back two answers.\n1545: The arms must agree, since the whole thing is one expression with one type.\n1546: \n1547: ## What the prototype showed\n1548: \n1549: A working compiler exists: `plans/` has the build order, `research-log/` has the findings, and\n1550: the language it accepts is the one described above minus the effect layer, object construction,\n1551: and everything listed under prototype 1's exclusions. It runs on three backends -- Lua,\n1552: JavaScript, and native through LLVM -- and a corpus of 22 programs is checked to produce\n1553: identical output on all three, with disagreement between backends counted as its own kind of\n1554: failure.\n1555: \n1556: That produced evidence for questions that had been argued rather than tested. Recorded here as\n1557: what happened, not as verdicts; the statuses in [the open questions table](plans/questions.md) are\n1558: still yours to move.\n1559: \n1560: ### Stream lowering does not block a backend that does not stream\n1561: \n1562: [The stream-lowering question](plans/questions.md#q5-stream-lowering-strategy-across-the-three-backends) was recorded as blocking all backend work, and its\n1563: detail said the strategy must be decided before any backend is written. Three backends are\n1564: written and it was never touched.\n\n(Showing lines 1505-1564 of 2470. Use offset=1565 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"\nIt found no defect. See\n[a backend that finds nothing is evidence only if it is different](research-log/a-backend-that-finds-nothing-is-evidence-only-if-it-is-different.md)\nfor what that is and is not worth.\n\n### Output needs no side effect\n\nA top-level `Str` already prints raw, so a string containing newlines is line-oriented output.\nWhat was missing was not a way to print but a way to build that string, which is `unlines`, a\npure conversion from `Vec<Str>`. FizzBuzz to a hundred is then an ordinary program:\n\n```\nunlines(\n    range(100) | map(. + 1) | map(\n        . % 15 == 0 -> \"FizzBuzz\" or\n        . % 3 == 0  -> \"Fizz\"     or\n        . % 5 == 0  -> \"Buzz\"     or\n        str(.)\n    )\n)","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-http-query-sugar-research/draft.md","text":"\nIt found no defect. See\n[a backend that finds nothing is evidence only if it is different](research-log/a-backend-that-finds-nothing-is-evidence-only-if-it-is-different.md)\nfor what that is and is not worth.\n\n### Output needs no side effect\n\nA top-level `Str` already prints raw, so a string containing newlines is line-oriented output.\nWhat was missing was not a way to print but a way to build that string, which is `unlines`, a\npure conversion from `Vec<Str>`. FizzBuzz to a hundred is then an ordinary program:\n\n```\nunlines(\n    range(100) | map(. + 1) | map(\n        . % 15 == 0 -> \"FizzBuzz\" or\n        . % 3 == 0  -> \"Fizz\"     or\n        . % 5 == 0  -> \"Buzz\"     or\n        str(.)\n    )\n)\n```\n\nAdding a `print` instead would have been the language's first side effect, and a much larger step\nthan it looks. There is no answer to what `map(print(...))` returns in an expression language;\nordering would become observable, which is exactly what stops a map being reordered or\nvectorised; and the effect would not be in the type, which is what principle 2 exists to prevent.\n\n`unlines` is named for Haskell's rather than being called `lines`, because `lines` is spoken for\nby the splitting direction that `stdin.lines` needs. The general `join(over, with)` cannot be\nwritten at all: functions are unary, so a second argument means passing a record, and there is no\nrecord literal to build one with. That is\n[a type you can declare but cannot build](research-log/a-type-you-can-declare-but-cannot-build.md)\nbiting somewhere a user would actually reach.\n\n`range(n)` is zero-based, matching jq, Python, and this language's own indices, which is why the\nexample shifts with `map(. + 1)` rather than starting at one.\n\n**A guard is exactly one `Bool`**, which is the claim\n[the safety section](#why-cardinality-in-the-type-is-the-safety-mechanism) makes: a match arm's\nleft side must be a Bool, where jq would run both branches of its `if` and hand back two answers.\nThe arms must agree, since the whole thing is one expression with one type.\n\n## What the prototype showed\n\nA working compiler exists: `plans/` has the build order, `research-log/` has the findings, and\nthe language it accepts is the one described above minus the effect layer, object construction,\nand everything listed under prototype 1's exclusions. It runs on three backends -- Lua,\nJavaScript, and native through LLVM -- and a corpus of 22 programs is checked to produce\nidentical output on all three, with disagreement between backends counted as its own kind of\nfailure.\n\nThat produced evidence for questions that had been argued rather than tested. Recorded here as\nwhat happened, not as verdicts; the statuses in [the open questions table](plans/questions.md) are\nstill yours to move.\n\n### Stream lowering does not block a backend that does not stream\n\n[The stream-lowering question](plans/questions.md#q5-stream-lowering-strategy-across-the-three-backends) was recorded as blocking all backend work, and its\ndetail said the strategy must be decided before any backend is written. Three backends are\nwritten and it was never touched.","lineStart":1505,"lineEnd":1564,"totalLines":2470,"truncated":true}},"title":"draft.md","time":{"start":1788297102579,"end":1788297102609}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me read the \"Output needs no side effect\" section (line 1510-1547) and the \"DECIDED: one stdin source is the destination\" section (line 1896). And the \"DECIDED: a minimal cut of streaming input, pull-based\" (1773). These are the sink/source decisions that the HTTP sugar would build on.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ed044ec001UWA6sT3Dg050Cr","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","messageID":"msg_05ed031cb001RN7IcI4Vtuce9M"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297102613,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_7c89ea5e4c244aa3a830be7d","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-http-query-sugar-research/draft.md","limit":75,"offset":1896},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-http-query-sugar-research/draft.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1896: ## DECIDED: one stdin source is the destination\n1897: \n1898: Direction settled 2026-08-28: the three stdin keywords (`input`, `inputs`, `lines`) are\n1899: officially transitional. The destination is one raw source with parsing as ordinary,\n1900: visible steps -- the design rejected earlier *only* because expected types could not flow\n1901: into map bodies, a blocker the type-flow rework removes. Syntax is deliberately not settled\n1902: here; it gets its own session once type-flow lands. Until then: no fourth stdin reader, no\n1903: new exclusivity rules -- nothing that makes the migration bigger. One ratified exception\n1904: (gh:88's ruling, built as gh:136): a parameterized `dsv(delim)` source, with `csv`/`tsv`\n1905: as its predefined partials -- the shape the one-source design lands on, added ahead of it\n1906: rather than as a fourth nullary keyword.\n1907: \n1908: ## DECIDED: `inputs`, eager, not an answer to Q1 either\n1909: \n1910: jq's own `inputs` -- every remaining JSON value on stdin, one per line -- was the obvious next\n1911: question once Field distributing over a Vec turned out to only need what CONTEXT.md's Spec\n1912: glossary already calls Keep and Narrow \"streamable\" for: an iterator, not storage. That\n1913: observation does not, on its own, get toylang a lazy `Stream<T>`,\n1914: because a program still has to terminate and return one value\n1915: ([Q35](plans/questions.md#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return) again): nothing can\n1916: consume a stream incrementally forever today, so the only thing genuine laziness would buy is\n1917: memory, not a new capability. `inputs` is eager instead: a bare keyword, typed `Vec<T>`, `T`\n1918: inferred from first use exactly the way `input`'s own type is -- read in full, parsed, collected,\n1919: before the program body runs. Ergonomics, not the general question; Q1 stays exactly as open as it\n1920: was.\n1921: \n1922: A more general alternative was considered first and rejected on a concrete, checkable ground\n1923: rather than taste: a `parse(s: Str) -> T` builtin, composing as `collect(lines) | map(parse(.))`,\n1924: would have been more reusable (also parsing a JSON string embedded in an ordinary field) and\n1925: would have needed no third stdin mode at all. It does not work with the checker as it stands.\n1926: `expect()` threads an expected type into exactly one form, `Expr::Input`; a `map` body is always\n1927: `synth`ed bottom-up, with nothing flowing in from whatever consumes the map's result. `parse(.)`\n1928: inside `map` has no expected type to resolve `T` against, the same hole an empty `[]` literal\n1929: falls into and for the same reason. Fixing that generally -- expected types flowing through `Map`,\n1930: at minimum -- is real, separate checker work, not scoped to this feature, and this would only be\n1931: its first forcing use case.\n1932: \n1933: All three stdin readers are now mutually exclusive, not just `input` and `lines` as before, and\n1934: the third leg was not a judgment call: Python's `input` reads all of stdin to EOF as one buffer\n1935: (`sys.stdin.buffer.read()`) before parsing, leaving nothing for `inputs` to read afterward even in\n1936: principle, and jq needs a different invocation flag for raw-line mode (`-R -n`) than for\n1937: parsed-JSON mode (`-n` alone) -- one process cannot run with both. `inputs` and `lines` could not\n1938: have coexisted on either backend regardless of what this document decided.\n1939: \n1940: Native's `tl_read_inputs` (`runtime/toylang.c`) reuses both existing pieces rather than needing a\n1941: new parser: the same `getline` loop `tl_collect_lines` already has for \"one line at a time,\" and\n1942: the same descriptor-driven `tl_parse` `tl_read_input` already has for \"one JSON value, this\n1943: shape\" -- `tl_parse` was already structured around an arbitrary buffer range, not \"all of stdin,\"\n1944: so looping it per line needed no changes to it at all. The one new piece of care: `tl_parse` hands\n1945: a record back as one packed blob, not columnar data, so `tl_read_inputs` spreads it into the\n1946: struct-of-arrays layout itself -- a fourth site for the invariant\n1947: [three other construction sites](research-log/one-invariant-three-independent-construction-sites.md)\n1948: each violated independently, verified here against a corpus case\n1949: (`tests/corpus/inputs_records.yaml`) rather than found the way those three were.\n1950: \n1951: Superseded on one point: the premise above, that nothing can consume a stream incrementally so\n1952: laziness would only buy memory, stopped being true when the fused `jsonlines(f(inputs))` loop\n1953: shipped. `inputs` is no longer typed `Vec<T>`; it is a source of `Stream<T>`, and eager\n1954: consumption is spelled `collect(inputs)`. See\n1955: [the streams decision](#decided-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed). The rejection of `parse`\n1956: and the three-way stdin exclusivity above are untouched by this.\n1957: \n1958: ## DECIDED: `jsonlines`, and the jq tutorial reproduces in full\n1959: \n1960: jq's own tutorial (jqlang.org/tutorial) has six filters. Five already matched real jq byte for\n1961: byte: `.`, `.[0]`, `.[0] | {...}`, `[.[] | {...}]`, and the full nested version with `parents`.\n1962: The sixth, `.[] | {message: ..., name: ...}`, printed as impossible for a real reason: jq's `.[]`\n1963: is lazy, so a later element's failure does not erase what already printed for earlier ones --\n1964: verified directly, `[1,2,0,3] | .[] | 10/.` in real jq prints `10` then `5` before erroring on the\n1965: zero. toylang's `Vec` is eager and whole, exactly as the streaming section above kept it: nothing\n1966: prints until everything exists.\n1967: \n1968: That was the truth about step 4's *failure* semantics, and it is still true. It was not, on\n1969: inspection, the truth about its *success* semantics. `jq -c` printing one JSON value per line and\n1970: `jq -c` printing one array are the same bytes with the brackets and commas swapped for\n\n(Showing lines 1896-1970 of 2470. Use offset=1971 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"## DECIDED: one stdin source is the destination\n\nDirection settled 2026-08-28: the three stdin keywords (`input`, `inputs`, `lines`) are\nofficially transitional. The destination is one raw source with parsing as ordinary,\nvisible steps -- the design rejected earlier *only* because expected types could not flow\ninto map bodies, a blocker the type-flow rework removes. Syntax is deliberately not settled\nhere; it gets its own session once type-flow lands. Until then: no fourth stdin reader, no\nnew exclusivity rules -- nothing that makes the migration bigger. One ratified exception\n(gh:88's ruling, built as gh:136): a parameterized `dsv(delim)` source, with `csv`/`tsv`\nas its predefined partials -- the shape the one-source design lands on, added ahead of it\nrather than as a fourth nullary keyword.\n\n## DECIDED: `inputs`, eager, not an answer to Q1 either\n\njq's own `inputs` -- every remaining JSON value on stdin, one per line -- was the obvious next\nquestion once Field distributing over a Vec turned out to only need what CONTEXT.md's Spec\nglossary already calls Keep and Narrow \"streamable\" for: an iterator, not storage. That\nobservation does not, on its own, get toylang a lazy `Stream<T>`,\nbecause a program still has to terminate and return one value\n([Q35](plans/questions.md#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return) again): nothing can","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-http-query-sugar-research/draft.md","text":"## DECIDED: one stdin source is the destination\n\nDirection settled 2026-08-28: the three stdin keywords (`input`, `inputs`, `lines`) are\nofficially transitional. The destination is one raw source with parsing as ordinary,\nvisible steps -- the design rejected earlier *only* because expected types could not flow\ninto map bodies, a blocker the type-flow rework removes. Syntax is deliberately not settled\nhere; it gets its own session once type-flow lands. Until then: no fourth stdin reader, no\nnew exclusivity rules -- nothing that makes the migration bigger. One ratified exception\n(gh:88's ruling, built as gh:136): a parameterized `dsv(delim)` source, with `csv`/`tsv`\nas its predefined partials -- the shape the one-source design lands on, added ahead of it\nrather than as a fourth nullary keyword.\n\n## DECIDED: `inputs`, eager, not an answer to Q1 either\n\njq's own `inputs` -- every remaining JSON value on stdin, one per line -- was the obvious next\nquestion once Field distributing over a Vec turned out to only need what CONTEXT.md's Spec\nglossary already calls Keep and Narrow \"streamable\" for: an iterator, not storage. That\nobservation does not, on its own, get toylang a lazy `Stream<T>`,\nbecause a program still has to terminate and return one value\n([Q35](plans/questions.md#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return) again): nothing can\nconsume a stream incrementally forever today, so the only thing genuine laziness would buy is\nmemory, not a new capability. `inputs` is eager instead: a bare keyword, typed `Vec<T>`, `T`\ninferred from first use exactly the way `input`'s own type is -- read in full, parsed, collected,\nbefore the program body runs. Ergonomics, not the general question; Q1 stays exactly as open as it\nwas.\n\nA more general alternative was considered first and rejected on a concrete, checkable ground\nrather than taste: a `parse(s: Str) -> T` builtin, composing as `collect(lines) | map(parse(.))`,\nwould have been more reusable (also parsing a JSON string embedded in an ordinary field) and\nwould have needed no third stdin mode at all. It does not work with the checker as it stands.\n`expect()` threads an expected type into exactly one form, `Expr::Input`; a `map` body is always\n`synth`ed bottom-up, with nothing flowing in from whatever consumes the map's result. `parse(.)`\ninside `map` has no expected type to resolve `T` against, the same hole an empty `[]` literal\nfalls into and for the same reason. Fixing that generally -- expected types flowing through `Map`,\nat minimum -- is real, separate checker work, not scoped to this feature, and this would only be\nits first forcing use case.\n\nAll three stdin readers are now mutually exclusive, not just `input` and `lines` as before, and\nthe third leg was not a judgment call: Python's `input` reads all of stdin to EOF as one buffer\n(`sys.stdin.buffer.read()`) before parsing, leaving nothing for `inputs` to read afterward even in\nprinciple, and jq needs a different invocation flag for raw-line mode (`-R -n`) than for\nparsed-JSON mode (`-n` alone) -- one process cannot run with both. `inputs` and `lines` could not\nhave coexisted on either backend regardless of what this document decided.\n\nNative's `tl_read_inputs` (`runtime/toylang.c`) reuses both existing pieces rather than needing a\nnew parser: the same `getline` loop `tl_collect_lines` already has for \"one line at a time,\" and\nthe same descriptor-driven `tl_parse` `tl_read_input` already has for \"one JSON value, this\nshape\" -- `tl_parse` was already structured around an arbitrary buffer range, not \"all of stdin,\"\nso looping it per line needed no changes to it at all. The one new piece of care: `tl_parse` hands\na record back as one packed blob, not columnar data, so `tl_read_inputs` spreads it into the\nstruct-of-arrays layout itself -- a fourth site for the invariant\n[three other construction sites](research-log/one-invariant-three-independent-construction-sites.md)\neach violated independently, verified here against a corpus case\n(`tests/corpus/inputs_records.yaml`) rather than found the way those three were.\n\nSuperseded on one point: the premise above, that nothing can consume a stream incrementally so\nlaziness would only buy memory, stopped being true when the fused `jsonlines(f(inputs))` loop\nshipped. `inputs` is no longer typed `Vec<T>`; it is a source of `Stream<T>`, and eager\nconsumption is spelled `collect(inputs)`. See\n[the streams decision](#decided-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed). The rejection of `parse`\nand the three-way stdin exclusivity above are untouched by this.\n\n## DECIDED: `jsonlines`, and the jq tutorial reproduces in full\n\njq's own tutorial (jqlang.org/tutorial) has six filters. Five already matched real jq byte for\nbyte: `.`, `.[0]`, `.[0] | {...}`, `[.[] | {...}]`, and the full nested version with `parents`.\nThe sixth, `.[] | {message: ..., name: ...}`, printed as impossible for a real reason: jq's `.[]`\nis lazy, so a later element's failure does not erase what already printed for earlier ones --\nverified directly, `[1,2,0,3] | .[] | 10/.` in real jq prints `10` then `5` before erroring on the\nzero. toylang's `Vec` is eager and whole, exactly as the streaming section above kept it: nothing\nprints until everything exists.\n\nThat was the truth about step 4's *failure* semantics, and it is still true. It was not, on\ninspection, the truth about its *success* semantics. `jq -c` printing one JSON value per line and\n`jq -c` printing one array are the same bytes with the brackets and commas swapped for","lineStart":1896,"lineEnd":1970,"totalLines":2470,"truncated":true}},"title":"draft.md","time":{"start":1788297102590,"end":1788297102611}},"id":"prt_05ed044f9001TlMsjlvJHCRO25","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","messageID":"msg_05ed031cb001RN7IcI4Vtuce9M"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788297102623,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"id":"prt_05ed0451d001jxoD5I3UP6qdPm","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"1da03e262f4e207599f9c8f9109adaed8aee925b","messageID":"msg_05ed031cb001RN7IcI4Vtuce9M","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":35640,"input":35371,"output":196,"reasoning":73,"cache":{"write":0,"read":0}},"cost":0.002347535}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788297103437,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"id":"prt_05ed04845001Hle1lq71uI01Cs","messageID":"msg_05ed04527001QtT9dRnhFwA4cj","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","snapshot":"1da03e262f4e207599f9c8f9109adaed8aee925b","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788297104723,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"id":"prt_05ed04c0b0016Bfel9AsGaEmOd","messageID":"msg_05ed04527001QtT9dRnhFwA4cj","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","type":"text","text":"\n\n","time":{"start":1788297104395,"end":1788297104720}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297104732,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_3cd8229cd9f7450d87f799d2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-http-query-sugar-research/draft.md","limit":125,"offset":1773},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-http-query-sugar-research/draft.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1773: ## DECIDED: a minimal cut of streaming input, pull-based, one new keyword\n1774: \n1775: Not an attempt to settle [Q1](plans/questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity)\n1776: in general. Q1 leans evaluation-level -- no `Stream<T>` type constructor, multiplicity born from\n1777: I/O and dying into values -- and that leaning is close to right but general enough to be\n1778: expensive to get wrong. What is decided here is the smallest concrete instance that neither\n1779: commits to nor contradicts it: one monomorphic, unparameterised primitive, not `Stream<T>` over\n1780: an arbitrary `T`.\n1781: \n1782: ### Pull, not push\n1783: \n1784: Every mature abstraction whose job is specifically \"do not let the producer outrun the consumer\"\n1785: turns out to be pull: Rust's `Iterator`, Python's generators, JavaScript's async iterators (the\n1786: layer people actually write against, even though the *raw* primitive underneath, `Readable` in\n1787: flowing mode, is push and is Node's own documented backpressure footgun), and jq itself --\n1788: verified empirically, `limit(3; range(100000000000))` returns three values instantly rather than\n1789: attempting to materialise an impossible sequence. Push-based designs need a second, explicit\n1790: protocol bolted on to get the same property (Node's `pause`/`resume`, Reactive Streams' `request`\n1791: demand signal); pull gets it as a consequence of the calling convention, for free.\n1792: \n1793: Two costs of this choice, named rather than discovered later. Fan-out -- using one stream in two\n1794: places -- is not supported; a pulled item is consumed once, and buffering it into a `Vec` first\n1795: (`collect`) is the only way to reuse it. Concurrency and overlap between pipeline stages are not\n1796: supported either; a pull chain does not run ahead of the consumer, so nothing overlaps with\n1797: anything. Both are named non-goals for this cut rather than gaps found later. What is not lost:\n1798: cross-process overlap, the kind `grep foo | wc -l` already gets from the kernel, is a property of\n1799: not pre-reading stdin into Rust before a subprocess backend runs, which this cut does -- an\n1800: architectural fact rather than a language feature.\n1801: \n1802: ### `lines`, `collect`, and nothing else exposed\n1803: \n1804: ```\n1805: unlines(collect(lines))\n1806: ```\n1807: \n1808: is the acceptance program: `cat`, echoing stdin back unchanged. `lines` is a bare keyword, zero\n1809: arguments, of one new monomorphic type with no spelling in the type grammar -- unlike `Str` or\n1810: `Int`, it cannot be a function's declared parameter or return type, which is what makes several\n1811: downstream checks unconditionally true rather than needing their own case: a `Lines` value can\n1812: never cross a function boundary, so a function body's synthesised type can never disagree with a\n1813: declared return type by containing one.\n1814: \n1815: No general `stdin` value is exposed. `input` (JSON, one document, read whole, validated before\n1816: any backend starts) is untouched, and a program may not use both: forced by jq specifically,\n1817: since raw-input mode (`-R`), needed for `[inputs]` to read lines rather than JSON, changes what\n1818: the whole invocation means, not just one call, and cannot coexist with `.`-is-the-document mode\n1819: in one run -- verified against real jq before the rule was written.\n1820: \n1821: `lines` may be referenced once, checked by the same mechanism that already caught `input` being\n1822: used at two disagreeing types, simplified to \"used at all, twice.\" Python's generators are the\n1823: cautionary example on the other side of this decision: consuming one twice silently returns\n1824: nothing, no error, and Rust's ownership model catching the equivalent mistake at compile time\n1825: (usually) is closer to what was chosen here on purpose.\n1826: \n1827: `Lines` is refused from a Vec, a record field, or the program's own printed result, checked at\n1828: three separate points rather than one, because a single \"does the top-level result contain\n1829: Lines\" scan does not catch every path: `collect([lines][0]!)` never makes Lines the program's own\n1830: result, but would have reached `to_slot`/`read_slot`/`go_type`'s struct-of-arrays or\n1831: closure-typing machinery with a type those never expected, on every backend, had the containment\n1832: check not been pushed back to where a Vec or record literal is built.\n1833: \n1834: ### The line rule, checked rather than assumed\n1835: \n1836: Split on `\\n` only. Strip it from each yielded string. No trailing empty entry when input ends in\n1837: `\\n`. The final line is still yielded even with none, which is deliberately not `wc -l`'s\n1838: mistake: `wc -l` counts newline characters rather than lines, so a file missing its last one is\n1839: undercounted -- verified directly, `printf 'a\\nb'` reports 1 rather than 2. A bare `\\r` is left\n1840: alone as ordinary content, matching `jq -R` and Python's own raw stdin iteration, neither of\n1841: which treats CRLF specially; Go's default `bufio.Scanner` split function does strip it, verified\n1842: directly, so the native Go split function is `bufio.ScanLines` with that one line removed rather\n1843: than the stdlib default.\n1844: \n1845: Every other backend's own native mechanism already agrees with the rule once verified: `getline`\n1846: (native), `io.lines()` (Lua, confirmed reading the real process stdin directly under `mlua`'s\n1847: default, unrestricted `Lua::new()`), raw `for line in sys.stdin` (Python), and a hand-written\n1848: `fs.readSync` chunk loop (JavaScript, which has no synchronous line reader built in -- the\n1849: well-known reason competitive-programming Node code writes this exact idiom by hand).\n1850: \n1851: ### What \"each backend reads for itself\" actually meant to build\n1852: \n1853: Two thirds of the six backends are separate OS processes Rust spawns (native, jq, Go, Python,\n1854: JavaScript); the toylang compiler is never a runtime dependency of what any of them emits, only\n1855: of producing it. The sixth, Lua, is not a process at all: `mlua` embeds the interpreter in the\n1856: same address space as the Rust harness, so \"reading stdin\" there is a function call, not IPC.\n1857: \n1858: For the five subprocesses, genuine streaming meant stopping Rust from reading all of stdin into a\n1859: `String` and re-`write_all`-ing it once, and instead connecting the real stdin straight through\n1860: (`Stdio::inherit()`) so the OS does the buffering it was always going to do. `main.rs`'s existing\n1861: gate -- read stdin only if `input` is used -- already did the right thing for `lines` by\n1862: accident, since neither construct needs Rust to touch the real stdin at all.\n1863: \n1864: What is fed depends on whether a caller supplied fixture text. `Some(text)` (a corpus case, most\n1865: often) is piped in verbatim and each backend does its own real splitting against it, which is the\n1866: only way the corpus can prove all six backends' splitting genuinely agrees rather than testing a\n1867: Rust-side reimplementation instead. `None` (the real command-line case) connects the live stdin.\n1868: Getting this distinction wrong the first time silently would have meant every corpus case for\n1869: this feature passing while testing nothing: the first version skipped writing fixture text to a\n1870: `lines` program's pipe entirely, matching `Stdio::inherit()`'s empty-until-corrected mental model\n1871: rather than the fixture's actual bytes, and would have reported every case as agreeing on an\n1872: empty string.\n1873: \n1874: Lua needed a different mechanism for the same distinction, since `Stdio` does not apply to a\n1875: function call in the same process, and `cargo test` runs many tests concurrently on shared\n1876: threads, which rules out redirecting the test process's own real stdin even temporarily. The\n1877: fixture is written to a file of its own, and the *global* `io.lines` function -- not `io.stdin`\n1878: -- is overridden to call the *real* `io.lines`, pointed at that file, so Lua's own line-splitting\n1879: still runs, verified rather than replaced by a Rust-side reimplementation feeding a pre-split\n1880: list.\n1881: \n1882: ### What this does not attempt\n1883: \n1884: No progressive output: a program still computes one final value and prints it once, unchanged\n1885: from every other program in the language. Real per-line output, and what a stream of *outputs*\n1886: would even mean, is [Q35](plans/questions.md#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return)'s\n1887: territory and stays there. No concurrency, no event loop, no `async`/`await` -- and not\n1888: having them is not merely smaller, it sidesteps two more named historical mistakes: JavaScript's\n1889: `Promise` starting eagerly with no cancellation designed in (irrelevant here, since nothing runs\n1890: until asked), and the sync/async colouring split that Rust and Python both still carry scars\n1891: from (irrelevant here, since there is only one calling convention). A future main loop, if one is\n1892: ever built, would not invalidate this: `stdin`/`lines` as a pull-shaped surface over an\n1893: event-loop-driven scheduler underneath is exactly how Python's `asyncio` and JavaScript's async\n1894: iterators are actually built, precedent rather than a hope.\n1895: \n1896: ## DECIDED: one stdin source is the destination\n1897: \n\n(Showing lines 1773-1897 of 2470. Use offset=1898 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"## DECIDED: a minimal cut of streaming input, pull-based, one new keyword\n\nNot an attempt to settle [Q1](plans/questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity)\nin general. Q1 leans evaluation-level -- no `Stream<T>` type constructor, multiplicity born from\nI/O and dying into values -- and that leaning is close to right but general enough to be\nexpensive to get wrong. What is decided here is the smallest concrete instance that neither\ncommits to nor contradicts it: one monomorphic, unparameterised primitive, not `Stream<T>` over\nan arbitrary `T`.\n\n### Pull, not push\n\nEvery mature abstraction whose job is specifically \"do not let the producer outrun the consumer\"\nturns out to be pull: Rust's `Iterator`, Python's generators, JavaScript's async iterators (the\nlayer people actually write against, even though the *raw* primitive underneath, `Readable` in\nflowing mode, is push and is Node's own documented backpressure footgun), and jq itself --\nverified empirically, `limit(3; range(100000000000))` returns three values instantly rather than\nattempting to materialise an impossible sequence. Push-based designs need a second, explicit\nprotocol bolted on to get the same property (Node's `pause`/`resume`, Reactive Streams' `request`\ndemand signal); pull gets it as a consequence of the calling convention, for free.\n","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-http-query-sugar-research/draft.md","text":"## DECIDED: a minimal cut of streaming input, pull-based, one new keyword\n\nNot an attempt to settle [Q1](plans/questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity)\nin general. Q1 leans evaluation-level -- no `Stream<T>` type constructor, multiplicity born from\nI/O and dying into values -- and that leaning is close to right but general enough to be\nexpensive to get wrong. What is decided here is the smallest concrete instance that neither\ncommits to nor contradicts it: one monomorphic, unparameterised primitive, not `Stream<T>` over\nan arbitrary `T`.\n\n### Pull, not push\n\nEvery mature abstraction whose job is specifically \"do not let the producer outrun the consumer\"\nturns out to be pull: Rust's `Iterator`, Python's generators, JavaScript's async iterators (the\nlayer people actually write against, even though the *raw* primitive underneath, `Readable` in\nflowing mode, is push and is Node's own documented backpressure footgun), and jq itself --\nverified empirically, `limit(3; range(100000000000))` returns three values instantly rather than\nattempting to materialise an impossible sequence. Push-based designs need a second, explicit\nprotocol bolted on to get the same property (Node's `pause`/`resume`, Reactive Streams' `request`\ndemand signal); pull gets it as a consequence of the calling convention, for free.\n\nTwo costs of this choice, named rather than discovered later. Fan-out -- using one stream in two\nplaces -- is not supported; a pulled item is consumed once, and buffering it into a `Vec` first\n(`collect`) is the only way to reuse it. Concurrency and overlap between pipeline stages are not\nsupported either; a pull chain does not run ahead of the consumer, so nothing overlaps with\nanything. Both are named non-goals for this cut rather than gaps found later. What is not lost:\ncross-process overlap, the kind `grep foo | wc -l` already gets from the kernel, is a property of\nnot pre-reading stdin into Rust before a subprocess backend runs, which this cut does -- an\narchitectural fact rather than a language feature.\n\n### `lines`, `collect`, and nothing else exposed\n\n```\nunlines(collect(lines))\n```\n\nis the acceptance program: `cat`, echoing stdin back unchanged. `lines` is a bare keyword, zero\narguments, of one new monomorphic type with no spelling in the type grammar -- unlike `Str` or\n`Int`, it cannot be a function's declared parameter or return type, which is what makes several\ndownstream checks unconditionally true rather than needing their own case: a `Lines` value can\nnever cross a function boundary, so a function body's synthesised type can never disagree with a\ndeclared return type by containing one.\n\nNo general `stdin` value is exposed. `input` (JSON, one document, read whole, validated before\nany backend starts) is untouched, and a program may not use both: forced by jq specifically,\nsince raw-input mode (`-R`), needed for `[inputs]` to read lines rather than JSON, changes what\nthe whole invocation means, not just one call, and cannot coexist with `.`-is-the-document mode\nin one run -- verified against real jq before the rule was written.\n\n`lines` may be referenced once, checked by the same mechanism that already caught `input` being\nused at two disagreeing types, simplified to \"used at all, twice.\" Python's generators are the\ncautionary example on the other side of this decision: consuming one twice silently returns\nnothing, no error, and Rust's ownership model catching the equivalent mistake at compile time\n(usually) is closer to what was chosen here on purpose.\n\n`Lines` is refused from a Vec, a record field, or the program's own printed result, checked at\nthree separate points rather than one, because a single \"does the top-level result contain\nLines\" scan does not catch every path: `collect([lines][0]!)` never makes Lines the program's own\nresult, but would have reached `to_slot`/`read_slot`/`go_type`'s struct-of-arrays or\nclosure-typing machinery with a type those never expected, on every backend, had the containment\ncheck not been pushed back to where a Vec or record literal is built.\n\n### The line rule, checked rather than assumed\n\nSplit on `\\n` only. Strip it from each yielded string. No trailing empty entry when input ends in\n`\\n`. The final line is still yielded even with none, which is deliberately not `wc -l`'s\nmistake: `wc -l` counts newline characters rather than lines, so a file missing its last one is\nundercounted -- verified directly, `printf 'a\\nb'` reports 1 rather than 2. A bare `\\r` is left\nalone as ordinary content, matching `jq -R` and Python's own raw stdin iteration, neither of\nwhich treats CRLF specially; Go's default `bufio.Scanner` split function does strip it, verified\ndirectly, so the native Go split function is `bufio.ScanLines` with that one line removed rather\nthan the stdlib default.\n\nEvery other backend's own native mechanism already agrees with the rule once verified: `getline`\n(native), `io.lines()` (Lua, confirmed reading the real process stdin directly under `mlua`'s\ndefault, unrestricted `Lua::new()`), raw `for line in sys.stdin` (Python), and a hand-written\n`fs.readSync` chunk loop (JavaScript, which has no synchronous line reader built in -- the\nwell-known reason competitive-programming Node code writes this exact idiom by hand).\n\n### What \"each backend reads for itself\" actually meant to build\n\nTwo thirds of the six backends are separate OS processes Rust spawns (native, jq, Go, Python,\nJavaScript); the toylang compiler is never a runtime dependency of what any of them emits, only\nof producing it. The sixth, Lua, is not a process at all: `mlua` embeds the interpreter in the\nsame address space as the Rust harness, so \"reading stdin\" there is a function call, not IPC.\n\nFor the five subprocesses, genuine streaming meant stopping Rust from reading all of stdin into a\n`String` and re-`write_all`-ing it once, and instead connecting the real stdin straight through\n(`Stdio::inherit()`) so the OS does the buffering it was always going to do. `main.rs`'s existing\ngate -- read stdin only if `input` is used -- already did the right thing for `lines` by\naccident, since neither construct needs Rust to touch the real stdin at all.\n\nWhat is fed depends on whether a caller supplied fixture text. `Some(text)` (a corpus case, most\noften) is piped in verbatim and each backend does its own real splitting against it, which is the\nonly way the corpus can prove all six backends' splitting genuinely agrees rather than testing a\nRust-side reimplementation instead. `None` (the real command-line case) connects the live stdin.\nGetting this distinction wrong the first time silently would have meant every corpus case for\nthis feature passing while testing nothing: the first version skipped writing fixture text to a\n`lines` program's pipe entirely, matching `Stdio::inherit()`'s empty-until-corrected mental model\nrather than the fixture's actual bytes, and would have reported every case as agreeing on an\nempty string.\n\nLua needed a different mechanism for the same distinction, since `Stdio` does not apply to a\nfunction call in the same process, and `cargo test` runs many tests concurrently on shared\nthreads, which rules out redirecting the test process's own real stdin even temporarily. The\nfixture is written to a file of its own, and the *global* `io.lines` function -- not `io.stdin`\n-- is overridden to call the *real* `io.lines`, pointed at that file, so Lua's own line-splitting\nstill runs, verified rather than replaced by a Rust-side reimplementation feeding a pre-split\nlist.\n\n### What this does not attempt\n\nNo progressive output: a program still computes one final value and prints it once, unchanged\nfrom every other program in the language. Real per-line output, and what a stream of *outputs*\nwould even mean, is [Q35](plans/questions.md#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return)'s\nterritory and stays there. No concurrency, no event loop, no `async`/`await` -- and not\nhaving them is not merely smaller, it sidesteps two more named historical mistakes: JavaScript's\n`Promise` starting eagerly with no cancellation designed in (irrelevant here, since nothing runs\nuntil asked), and the sync/async colouring split that Rust and Python both still carry scars\nfrom (irrelevant here, since there is only one calling convention). A future main loop, if one is\never built, would not invalidate this: `stdin`/`lines` as a pull-shaped surface over an\nevent-loop-driven scheduler underneath is exactly how Python's `asyncio` and JavaScript's async\niterators are actually built, precedent rather than a hope.\n\n## DECIDED: one stdin source is the destination\n","lineStart":1773,"lineEnd":1897,"totalLines":2470,"truncated":true}},"title":"draft.md","time":{"start":1788297104713,"end":1788297104730}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the source/sink vocabulary well:\n- Sources: `input` (single JSON value from stdin), `inputs` (stream of JSON values, one per line), `lines` (raw lines stream), `dsv(delim)` (parameterized).\n- Sink: the program returns one value, which is printed (top-level Str prints raw; other values print as JSON).\n- Output needs no side effect: a print builtin was rejected.\n- `stdin` and `stdout` are byte streams with no element type.\n\nNow let me look at the docs directory for the reference pages describing the language. Let me check what reference pages exist, and also read the \"DECIDED: minimal cut of streaming input\" section 1773-1882 for the pull-based model.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ed04d40001TM5NvgQAgAcGtd","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","messageID":"msg_05ed04527001QtT9dRnhFwA4cj"}}
