{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297107558,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_3d1d9e2f5e364a9abcfdb5e1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -rn \"fn emit\\|//! \\|Backend\\|backend\" src/emit_go.rs src/emit_js.rs src/emit_py.rs src/emit_lua.rs src/emit_rs.rs src/emit_jq.rs src/emit_llvm.rs | grep -i \"backend\\|//!\" | head -50"},"output":"src/emit_go.rs:1://! The Go backend.\nsrc/emit_go.rs:3://! Go is the first target that is both statically typed and has no runtime type information to\nsrc/emit_go.rs:4://! fall back on, and that changes what an emitter has to do. Lua, JavaScript and jq all accept a\nsrc/emit_go.rs:5://! depth-polymorphic helper -- one `tl_field(v, k, depth)` serves every shape -- because the\nsrc/emit_go.rs:6://! value carries its own type. LLVM sidesteps the same problem by erasing everything into\nsrc/emit_go.rs:7://! `tl_vec`. Go will not do either: a `[]User` and a `[][]User` are different types, so the\nsrc/emit_go.rs:8://! distribution over dimensions has to be spelled out at each use site, with the intermediate\nsrc/emit_go.rs:9://! types written in. The checker computed those types already, so what looks like extra work is\nsrc/emit_go.rs:10://! really the first target that asks for what was always there.\nsrc/emit_go.rs:12://! The second thing Go asks for is a *name*. A record type is structural here and anonymous\nsrc/emit_go.rs:13://! everywhere else; Go needs a declared struct before a value of one can exist, so every record\nsrc/emit_go.rs:14://! the program mentions is collected up front and given one.\nsrc/emit_go.rs:184:/// wrapping answer the other backends were made to give.\nsrc/emit_go.rs:241:// bufio.ScanLines strips a trailing \\r along with the \\n, which the other five backends do\nsrc/emit_go.rs:366:/// different string than the other four backends for the same value.\nsrc/emit_go.rs:992:    /// This is what the other backends get for free from a `depth` argument. Here the map at\nsrc/emit_go.rs:1108:                // Go, so they cannot share one helper the way a dynamically typed backend can.\nsrc/emit_go.rs:1289:    /// backend. Here there is no choice at all: a Go value cannot be asked what it is.\nsrc/emit_go.rs:1423:/// backend where the wrapping rule costs nothing to state -- and the width changes nothing\nsrc/emit_js.rs:228:    // forward declarations Lua needs. Each backend does what its own target does.\nsrc/emit_js.rs:376:/// of disagreement where one backend enumerates keys in insertion order and another sorts\nsrc/emit_js.rs:377:/// them, and it is what a native backend will have to do anyway, having no runtime type\nsrc/emit_js.rs:822:            // The one real conversion among the backends: an Int is a number and an Int64 is\nsrc/emit_py.rs:1://! The Python backend.\nsrc/emit_py.rs:3://! Python is dynamically typed, so the depth-polymorphic helpers that Go could not have are\nsrc/emit_py.rs:4://! available again: one `tl_field(v, k, depth)` serves every shape. What it stresses instead is\nsrc/emit_py.rs:5://! arithmetic, from a direction none of the other five come from. Its integers are exact and\nsrc/emit_py.rs:6://! unbounded, so wrapping is emulation like jq's -- but unlike jq nothing is lost on the way,\nsrc/emit_py.rs:7://! so the whole rule is one modulo rather than a split into 16-bit halves. Its `//` and `%` are\nsrc/emit_py.rs:8://! floored, so truncated division needs writing out.\nsrc/emit_py.rs:10://! One mapping is better here than anywhere else: a record is a dict, which is also what\nsrc/emit_py.rs:11://! `json.loads` produces, so reading input is the parse and nothing more. Go needed a declared\nsrc/emit_py.rs:12://! struct and a decoder to reach the same place.\nsrc/emit_py.rs:130:/// decodes by codepoint; `ord` is the cast down to the number every other backend represents a\nsrc/emit_py.rs:243:        // started under. The native backend writes bytes for the same reason.\nsrc/emit_py.rs:361:/// The printer is built from the type rather than by inspecting the value, as on every backend.\nsrc/emit_lua.rs:150:// backend's ordering on both numbers and (byte-wise, so codepoint-wise for valid UTF-8) strings,\nsrc/emit_lua.rs:243:// `*` need nothing at all -- the one backend where the wider type is cheaper than the narrow\nsrc/emit_lua.rs:447:/// asking it was always going to disagree with a backend that knows. See emit_js for the same\nsrc/emit_lua.rs:480:            // built by joining nothing. The other backends survive this by construction.\nsrc/emit_rs.rs:1://! The Rust backend.\nsrc/emit_rs.rs:3://! Closer to Go than to any other target: both are statically typed with no runtime type\nsrc/emit_rs.rs:4://! information, so field distribution over a Vec has to be spelled out at each depth the same\nsrc/emit_rs.rs:5://! way, and a record needs a declared struct before a value of one can exist. Rust gets three\nsrc/emit_rs.rs:6://! things Go does not: a real `Option<T>` (no `tlOpt[T]` wrapper needed), a real conditional\nsrc/emit_rs.rs:7://! expression (`if`/`else` needs no closure the way Go's does), and expression-level `let` (a\nsrc/emit_rs.rs:8://! pipe is a block, not an immediately-invoked closure).\nsrc/emit_rs.rs:10://! No external crate. `rustc` compiles one self-contained file, the same way `cc` compiles\nsrc/emit_rs.rs:11://! native's, so a built program depends on nothing this compiler did not already assume; JSON\nsrc/emit_rs.rs:12://! reading and writing are both hand-written here for the same reason every other backend's are.\n","metadata":{"output":"src/emit_go.rs:1://! The Go backend.\nsrc/emit_go.rs:3://! Go is the first target that is both statically typed and has no runtime type information to\nsrc/emit_go.rs:4://! fall back on, and that changes what an emitter has to do. Lua, JavaScript and jq all accept a\nsrc/emit_go.rs:5://! depth-polymorphic helper -- one `tl_field(v, k, depth)` serves every shape -- because t