{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788297128640,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"id":"prt_05ed0aabe001bY2Zphy5fsTJSM","messageID":"msg_05ed0a475001imQKBBtnZcsNG7","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","snapshot":"1da03e262f4e207599f9c8f9109adaed8aee925b","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297129784,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-be0ede8da426ced5","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-http-query-sugar-research/plans/erlang-target-research.md","limit":60},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-http-query-sugar-research/plans/erlang-target-research.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Erlang as a target: what its semantics would actually demand\n2: \n3: Desk research for issue #163, which floats Erlang as a candidate backend whose\n4: point would be to stress test the design. The question here is narrower than \"would\n5: this be nice to have\". The project's admission test for a backend\n6: ([ADR 0002](../docs/adr/0002-backends-as-falsifiers.md), and the research note\n7: [a backend that finds nothing is evidence only if it is different](../research-log/a-backend-that-finds-nothing-is-evidence-only-if-it-is-different.md))\n8: is which axis a candidate is unlike the existing ones on. Everything below is a\n9: documented-semantics comparison; no Erlang toolchain was consulted or run.\n10: \n11: The short version of the finding: Erlang is unlike every existing backend on exactly\n12: one axis, concurrency and message passing, and toylang has no construct that would\n13: exercise that axis. On every axis the language actually uses, Erlang duplicates an\n14: existing witness. As a shipped backend it fails the admission test. As a *thought\n15: experiment* against the open questions, it is the most opinionated thing available,\n16: and two of its features (selective receive, process-as-stream) sit directly on open\n17: design questions. So the stress test is worth doing, but it is a research exercise,\n18: not a backend.\n19: \n20: ## Erlang's model, in the vocabulary the design already uses\n21: \n22: Erlang is a dynamically typed, single-assignment language whose concurrency is the\n23: organizing principle rather than a library. Four facts matter here, each of which is\n24: a claim about toylang vocabulary.\n25: \n26: **Terms are immutable, nested data.** An Erlang term is an atom, integer, float,\n27: binary, tuple, or list, composed arbitrarily. There is no mutation; a variable binds\n28: once per clause and then names a value forever. That is toylang's value model minus\n29: the JSON shapes: an atom is a bare-string enum variant or a string, a tuple is a\n30: record or a single-key enum wrapper, a list is a `Vec`, a binary is a string. The\n31: nested term is exactly the canonical JSON value, rendered in Erlang's own notation.\n32: \n33: **Pattern matching is the central operation, and it is single-assignment.** Erlang\n34: dispatches on shape everywhere: function clauses are tried in order and the first\n35: whose head matches wins, `case` and `receive` do the same. A match binds each\n36: variable at most once, compares literals, and walks nested structure; there is no\n37: backtracking and no unification of one match's bindings with another's. This is\n38: toylang's ordered arm list with the lens-style decode left out.\n39: \n40: **Processes communicate by message passing.** A process has a mailbox. It can\n41: `send` a term to another process's identifier and `receive` messages, and here is\n42: the feature with no toylang analogue: `receive` is *selective*. A process writes a\n43: list of message patterns and the runtime scans the mailbox for the first message\n44: matching any of them; messages that match nothing are left in place for a later\n45: receive. Order of arrival is not order of consumption.\n46: \n47: **There is no type system and no effect system.** Dialyzer infers success typings\n48: as an optional analysis, but the language itself has no compile-time types, no\n49: cardinality, and no notion of an effect. Sending, spawning, and I/O are ordinary\n50: operations with no tracking. A missing clause or a message no pattern matches is a\n51: runtime `function_clause` or `case_clause` error, not a check.\n52: \n53: ## Where toylang maps cleanly\n54: \n55: The mapping is clean exactly where Erlang duplicates a witness the project already\n56: has.\n57: \n58: **Ordered arm matching is the same machinery as Erlang clause matching.** Toylang's\n59: matcher is first-match-wins over an ordered arm list with guards, an `or`\n60: composition, and a `partial` flag that types an honest chain as `Opt`\n\n(Showing lines 1-60 of 177. Use offset=61 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Erlang as a target: what its semantics would actually demand\n\nDesk research for issue #163, which floats Erlang as a candidate backend whose\npoint would be to stress test the design. The question here is narrower than \"would\nthis be nice to have\". The project's admission test for a backend\n([ADR 0002](../docs/adr/0002-backends-as-falsifiers.md), and the research note\n[a backend that finds nothing is evidence only if it is different](../research-log/a-backend-that-finds-nothing-is-evidence-only-if-it-is-different.md))\nis which axis a candidate is unlike the existing ones on. Everything below is a\ndocumented-semantics comparison; no Erlang toolchain was consulted or run.\n\nThe short version of the finding: Erlang is unlike every existing backend on exactly\none axis, concurrency and message passing, and toylang has no construct that would\nexercise that axis. On every axis the language actually uses, Erlang duplicates an\nexisting witness. As a shipped backend it fails the admission test. As a *thought\nexperiment* against the open questions, it is the most opinionated thing available,\nand two of its features (selective receive, process-as-stream) sit directly on open\ndesign questions. So the stress test is worth doing, but it is a research exercise,\nnot a backend.\n\n## Erlang's model, in the vocabulary the design already uses","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-http-query-sugar-research/plans/erlang-target-research.md","text":"# Erlang as a target: what its semantics would actually demand\n\nDesk research for issue #163, which floats Erlang as a candidate backend whose\npoint would be to stress test the design. The question here is narrower than \"would\nthis be nice to have\". The project's admission test for a backend\n([ADR 0002](../docs/adr/0002-backends-as-falsifiers.md), and the research note\n[a backend that finds nothing is evidence only if it is different](../research-log/a-backend-that-finds-nothing-is-evidence-only-if-it-is-different.md))\nis which axis a candidate is unlike the existing ones on. Everything below is a\ndocumented-semantics comparison; no Erlang toolchain was consulted or run.\n\nThe short version of the finding: Erlang is unlike every existing backend on exactly\none axis, concurrency and message passing, and toylang has no construct that would\nexercise that axis. On every axis the language actually uses, Erlang duplicates an\nexisting witness. As a shipped backend it fails the admission test. As a *thought\nexperiment* against the open questions, it is the most opinionated thing available,\nand two of its features (selective receive, process-as-stream) sit directly on open\ndesign questions. So the stress test is worth doing, but it is a research exercise,\nnot a backend.\n\n## Erlang's model, in the vocabulary the design already uses\n\nErlang is a dynamically typed, single-assignment language whose concurrency is the\norganizing principle rather than a library. Four facts matter here, each of which is\na claim about toylang vocabulary.\n\n**Terms are immutable, nested data.** An Erlang term is an atom, integer, float,\nbinary, tuple, or list, composed arbitrarily. There is no mutation; a variable binds\nonce per clause and then names a value forever. That is toylang's value model minus\nthe JSON shapes: an atom is a bare-string enum variant or a string, a tuple is a\nrecord or a single-key enum wrapper, a list is a `Vec`, a binary is a string. The\nnested term is exactly the canonical JSON value, rendered in Erlang's own notation.\n\n**Pattern matching is the central operation, and it is single-assignment.** Erlang\ndispatches on shape everywhere: function clauses are tried in order and the first\nwhose head matches wins, `case` and `receive` do the same. A match binds each\nvariable at most once, compares literals, and walks nested structure; there is no\nbacktracking and no unification of one match's bindings with another's. This is\ntoylang's ordered arm list with the lens-style decode left out.\n\n**Processes communicate by message passing.** A process has a mailbox. It can\n`send` a term to another process's identifier and `receive` messages, and here is\nthe feature with no toylang analogue: `receive` is *selective*. A process writes a\nlist of message patterns and the runtime scans the mailbox for the first message\nmatching any of them; messages that match nothing are left in place for a later\nreceive. Order of arrival is not order of consumption.\n\n**There is no type system and no effect system.** Dialyzer infers success typings\nas an optional analysis, but the language itself has no compile-time types, no\ncardinality, and no notion of an effect. Sending, spawning, and I/O are ordinary\noperations with no tracking. A missing clause or a message no pattern matches is a\nruntime `function_clause` or `case_clause` error, not a check.\n\n## Where toylang maps cleanly\n\nThe mapping is clean exactly where Erlang duplicates a witness the project already\nhas.\n\n**Ordered arm matching is the same machinery as Erlang clause matching.** Toylang's\nmatcher is first-match-wins over an ordered arm list with guards, an `or`\ncomposition, and a `partial` flag that types an honest chain as `Opt`","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":60,"totalLines":177,"truncated":true}},"title":"plans/erlang-target-research.md","time":{"start":1788297129776,"end":1788297129782}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I have a solid understanding now. Let me look at one existing research spike file to match the format conventions, e.g. erlang-target-research.md. Let me read its beginning.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ed0adba0010iYrpW4txIF64a","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","messageID":"msg_05ed0a475001imQKBBtnZcsNG7"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788297129802,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"id":"prt_05ed0af47001G4ReQT85Wj5Mf3","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"1da03e262f4e207599f9c8f9109adaed8aee925b","messageID":"msg_05ed0a475001imQKBBtnZcsNG7","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":47738,"input":1271,"output":87,"reasoning":44,"cache":{"write":0,"read":46336}},"cost":0.000847571}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788297130823,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"id":"prt_05ed0b341001dGdjSxWg96rrCO","messageID":"msg_05ed0af520014RhqsWEjvdagO0","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","snapshot":"1da03e262f4e207599f9c8f9109adaed8aee925b","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788297151419,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"id":"prt_05ed1019f001bP3RZiw0JCWv97","messageID":"msg_05ed0af520014RhqsWEjvdagO0","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","type":"text","text":"\n\n","time":{"start":1788297150879,"end":1788297151418}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297151422,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_4de9245762544432927bad03","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-http-query-sugar-research/tests/corpus/div_by_zero.yaml"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-http-query-sugar-research/tests/corpus/div_by_zero.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # A zero divisor is the only way arithmetic can fail.\n2: program: |\n3:   str(1 / 0)\n4: \n5: refuses: true\n6: \n7: node_types: [arith.div, builtin.str, int]\n\n(End of file - total 7 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# A zero divisor is the only way arithmetic can fail.\nprogram: |\n  str(1 / 0)\n\nrefuses: true\n\nnode_types: [arith.div, builtin.str, int]","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-http-query-sugar-research/tests/corpus/div_by_zero.yaml","text":"# A zero divisor is the only way arithmetic can fail.\nprogram: |\n  str(1 / 0)\n\nrefuses: true\n\nnode_types: [arith.div, builtin.str, int]","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":7,"totalLines":7,"truncated":false}},"title":"tests/corpus/div_by_zero.yaml","time":{"start":1788297151403,"end":1788297151421}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Good. Now I understand the format: a research file with a title, context, findings grounded in the vocabulary, with real toylang code examples.\n\nNow let me check how the backends would actually provide HTTP. The board row says \"survey what request/response building blocks already exist per backend (Go net/http, JS fetch, Python urllib, Lua, Rust reqwest, native)\". Let me survey these. The task says \"Survey Python's requests library for the minimal realistic surface (get/post, headers, body, response status/body access)\".\n\nLet me also check how backends run: Python runs via python3 subprocess; Go via go run; JS via node; Lua embedded via mlua; Rust via rustc; native via cc (LLVM). jq via jq. Note: the Rust backend emits a self-contained file with hand-written JSON reading/writing, no external crates. And the native backend is C. For HTTP, these would need network client code.\n\nImportant: The compiler is run in offline test environments. The corpus runs every case on every backend, and they must agree. Adding HTTP would make network calls, which is a big deal for tests. But the task is a research spike — no code changes needed. So I just design syntax candidates.\n\n\n\nLet me survey the minimal requests surface from knowledge:\n- requests.get(url, params=None, headers=None, ...) -> Response\n- requests.post(url, data=None, json=None, headers=None, ...) -> Response\n- Response: .status_code, .text, .json(), .headers\n- Headers: dict of str -> str.\n\nThe minimal realistic surface: get/post, headers, body (request body), response status/body.\n\n Also maybe params (query string）。 \n\nNow I need 2-3 concrete toylang syntax candidates, each grounded in the current sink/source vocabulary. Let me think about what toylang vocabulary exists:\n\nExisting vocabulary:\n- Sources: `input`, `inputs`, `lines`, `dsv(delim)` — all stdin-based, read-only, born outside the program. The language is pull-based streaming for input.\n\n- Functions are unary: a record is how several arguments travel (decided). A record is built with `{}` literal. Argument parens optional when the argument is a record literal.\n\n- Output needs no side effect: a top-level value prints itself. There is no print builtin; a program computes one final value and prints it once.\n\n- `Vec<T>` eager; `Stream<T>` effect-layer, indexing only by iteration; `collect` collapses a stream into a Vec.\n\n- Records are the way several arguments travel; `map` is the only thing that crosses a dimension.\n\n\n\nNow how would HTTP fit? The maintainer wants \"thin sugar over whatever each backend runtime already provides\". The composition question: how does it compose with the existing pipe/sink syntax?\n\nKey design tension: HTTP is an effect — a request has side effects (network访问)., but toylang deliberately rejected `print` as the first side effect and the language computes one final value. However, the maintainer's note says \"consider a simple HTTP query builtin (like Python requests) as thin sugar over whatever each backend runtime already provides, minimal added complexity\". So the language would need a first side effect OR treat HTTP as a source/query primitive.\n\n\n\nThe design candidates should be grounded in the current sink/source vocabulary. Let me think of candidates:\n\nCandidate A: HTTP as a source keyword, analogous to `lines`/`inputs`: e.g., a `get(url)` builtin that returns the response body (parsed as JSON, like `input` does) — with a record argument for headers. Something like:\n\n```\nget(\"https://api.example.com/users\", {headers: {\"Authorization\": \"Bearer ...\"}})\n```\n\nResponse: what does it return? If it returns the body parsed as JSON, then it composes with everything downstream as a value: `get(url) | .[].name` etc. That is the cleanest composition: the result is an ordinary value (JSON parsed), exactly like `input`. For status and headers access you'd need a response object.\n\nCandidate B: a two-step shape mirroring `input`/`inputs`: HTTP introduces a new source type, maybe `fetch(url)` returning a response record `{status, headers, body}` and the body is parsed as JSON. Then you compose with field access: `fetch(url)..body | ...`. But that makes the body a JSON value inside a record, which the language supports.\n\n\n\nCandidate C: function-style with a record argument, mirroring how multiple arguments travel (the records decision): `http.get({url: ..., headers: ...})` or `get({url: ..., headers: ...})`. The response could be the parsed body or a response record.\n\n\n\nLet me think more carefully about what \"grounded in the current sink/source vocabulary\" means. The current vocabulary:\n- `input`: one JSON document, read whole, validated before any backend starts. Type is T inferred from use.\n- `inputs`: stream of JSON docs, one per line. Eager (now stream source)。\n- `lines`: raw lines, pull-based stream.\n- `dsv(delim)`: parameterized source.\n- A program has ONE stdin source (destination: one raw source with parsing as ordinary steps)。 There is a decision: \"DECIDED: one stdin source is the destination\" — syntax deliberately not settled; dsv is the ratified exception (a parameterized `dsv(delim)` source). So a precedent exists for parameterized sources beyond nullary keywords: `dsv(\";\")`. That's actually a very strong precedent: an HTTP query could be a parameterized source like `dsv`, e.g. `http(\"https://...\")` — but an HTTP response isn't a stream of lines/JSON values necessarily... although a JSON-lines API is exactly the `jsonlines`/`inputs` shape! So `http(url)` could be a source yielding the response body as parsed JSON value (or stream of values if the response is JSON-lines)。\n\nActually — the JSON-lines/http-sse pattern is real: HTTP APIs returning streams of JSON docs. The `inputs`/`jsonlines` vocabulary already handles that shape when it comes from stdin. An HTTP source could reuse the exact same downstream machinery: `collect(http(\"...\"))` vs `collect(inputs)`. That's beautifully grounded. A GET returns one JSON document → behaves like `input`. \n\nHmm, but a response has status code and headers. The minimal requests surface includes response status/body access. So maybe the response should be a record `{status, headers, body}` — but then downstream composition (e.g. `.[].name` on a JSON array body) needs unwrapping into the body first. Options:\n1. `http(url)` yields the parsed body (JSON value), erroring on non-2xx or on bad JSON — the minimal sugar, matching `input`'s behavior (input parses and validates). Status/headers unavailable or via a separate variant.\n\n2. `http(url)` yields a response record `{status: Int, headers: {...}, body: <parsed>}` — full minimal surface, but every query then needs `.body` to get at the data, and the body type is unknown (generic).\n3. Two entry points: `get(url)` (body-only sugar) and `get_resp(url)`/`fetch(url)` (full response record). That's two names, more surface.\n\n\n\nNow let me think about the actual ask: \"minimal HTTP query support (Python-requests-like: get/post, headers, body) as sugar over whatever backend already provides.\" The minimal surface per gh:171: get/post?, headers?, body?. So both get and post, headers (request headers?, response headers?), body (request body for POST, response body).\n\n\n\nLet me survey backends for what exists (from knowledge, since no networking code exists in repo):\n- Go: net/http package — http.Get(url), http.NewRequest, client.Do. Response: resp.StatusCode, resp.Header, resp.Body.\n\n- JS (node): global fetch (Node 18+), no external dependency. Response: resp.status, resp.headers, resp.text()/resp.json().\n\n\n- Python: urllib.request.urlopen(request) — stdlib, no external deps. Or requests (third-party; not stdlib).. The maintainer says \"like Python's requests\" as a UX reference, not a target: the Python backend would use urllib.request, which needs a Request object for method/headers/body: `urllib.request.Request(url, data, headers, method=...)`. Response: resp.status, resp.read()→ bytes, json.loads.\n\n\n\n- Lua: no stdlib HTTP. mlua embeds the interpreter; socket.http is a LuaSocket (external, C library).. Native Lua has no network. This is a real gap: the Lua backend currently has zero networking capability available without external deps. \"Whatever each backend runtime already provides\" — Lua provides nothing. So an HTTP builtin would have to either add a dependency (LuaSocket,) or refuse on Lua. The corpus's `refuses: true` mechanism is exactly how cross-backend disagreement is modeled: a case that asks for nothing... \"what each says while refusing is its own business\". So HTTP cases would likely be `refuses: true` on Lua (and maybe native).\n\n\n- Rust backend: hand-written JSON, no external crates (\"No external crate. rustc compiles one self-contained file... so a built program depends on nothing this compiler did not already assume\"). So HTTP on Rust would need hand-written TCP/HTTP client or an external crate — the constraint says no external crates. Rust stdlib has no HTTP. So Rust backend would need to hand-write a minimal HTTP client (doable: a minimal GET/POST over TcpStream — but TLS is the killer: https needs TLS, which stdlib lacks. So only plain http:// would be feasible on Rust without deps. Same for native C. TLS needs OpenSSL or similar. So realistic: only http:// URLs work on Rust/native/Lua backends; https requires a TLS lib per backend. Hmm — but wait, Go/Python/Node all ship TLS in stdlib (Go net/http supports https natively; Python ssl is stdlib; Node fetch does TLS). Rust stdlib: no; native C: no. This is a significant finding for the research: a real HTTP feature is https-heavy in practice, and only 3 of 6 backends can do https without external deps. That strongly shapes the recommendation: maybe the sugar should be scoped to backends that can do it (Go, Python, JS), with `refuses: true` elsewhere — but that is a big split. Or: bite the bullet and add a per-backend TLS dep. That's a maintainer decision; the research should surface it.\n\n\n\n- jq: no HTTP builtin in jq 1.7 (well, jq has no network builtins at all in the base binary). There is no HTTP in jq stdlib. So jq also refuses.\n\n- LLVM/native: C — needs a TLS library or plain http hand-rolled TCP.\n\nSo the realistic target surface: Go, Python, JS backends can do https natively with zero new deps; Lua, jq, native, Rust cannot (unless TLS deps are added or only plain-http supported).. This is the \"which backend(s) it targets\" question the maintainer flagged. The research should answer: target Go/Python/JS; refuse elsewhere, per the corpus's existing `refuses: true` mechanism (div_by_zero etc. are refusals).\n\nWait, let me verify refusals: does refusals: true exist for cross-backend? Yes — \"Every case runs on every backend... `refuses: true` means they all decline to run it\". Hmm, actually it says \"they all decline to run it\" — all backends decline. Let me check what div_by_zero looks like to see how refusal is expressed.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ed103a3001WRIRCgT4Z3814d","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","messageID":"msg_05ed0af520014RhqsWEjvdagO0"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297151426,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_cf6b257924454345802c89c6","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -rln \"refuses\" tests/corpus/ | head; echo ---; grep -rn \"refuses\" tests/corpus/div_by_zero.yaml tests/corpus/enum_recursive_walk.yaml tests/corpus/enum_inputs_reject.yaml 2>/dev/null"},"output":"tests/corpus/div_by_zero.yaml\ntests/corpus/enum_inputs_reject.yaml\ntests/corpus/enum_recursive_walk.yaml\ntests/corpus/enum_scalar_input_reject.yaml\ntests/corpus/field_key_case_is_significant.yaml\ntests/corpus/max_empty_unwrap_refuses.yaml\ntests/corpus/opt_unwrap_of_none_refuses.yaml\ntests/corpus/opt_unwrap_peels_one_level.yaml\ntests/corpus/rem_by_zero.yaml\ntests/corpus/tail_of_empty_refuses.yaml\n---\ntests/corpus/div_by_zero.yaml:5:refuses: true\ntests/corpus/enum_recursive_walk.yaml:7:# and refuses a cycle between two named functions.\ntests/corpus/enum_inputs_reject.yaml:15:refuses: true\n","metadata":{"output":"tests/corpus/div_by_zero.yaml\ntests/corpus/enum_inputs_reject.yaml\ntests/corpus/enum_recursive_walk.yaml\ntests/corpus/enum_scalar_input_reject.yaml\ntests/corpus/field_key_case_is_significant.yaml\ntests/corpus/max_empty_unwrap_refuses.yaml\ntests/corpus/opt_unwrap_of_none_refuses.yaml\ntests/corpus/opt_unwrap_peels_one_level.yaml\ntests/corpus/rem_by_zero.yaml\ntests/corpus/tail_of_empty_refuses.yaml\n---\ntests/corpus/div_by_zero.yaml:5:refuses: true\ntests/corpus/enum_recursive_walk.yaml:7:# and refuses a cycle between two named functions.\ntests/corpus/enum_inputs_reject.yaml:15:refuses: true\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -rln \"refuses\" tests/corpus/ | head; echo ---; grep -rn \"refuses\" tests/corpus/div_by_zero.yaml tests/corpus/enum_recursive_walk.yaml tests/corpus/enum_inputs_reject.yaml 2>/dev/null","time":{"start":1788297151424,"end":1788297151425}},"id":"prt_05ed103b1001M3i367wLmuKcfb","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","messageID":"msg_05ed0af520014RhqsWEjvdagO0"}}
