{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297163685,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8f1620069615fa4d","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"ls docs/reference/ | head -40"},"output":"builtins\noperators\nprelude\nsources\nsyntax\ntypes\n","metadata":{"output":"builtins\noperators\nprelude\nsources\nsyntax\ntypes\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"ls docs/reference/ | head -40","time":{"start":1788297163671,"end":1788297163682}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"So `refuses: true` means a case that compiles but fails at runtime (div by zero) — \"what each says while refusing is its own business\". These refusals are runtime failures that all backends reproduce. For HTTP, a case that only runs on some backends (Go/Python/JS) wouldn't be expressible as a corpus case at all (all backends must agree). But that's an implementation concern, not the research focus. The research focuses on syntax candidates.\n\nLet me check the docs reference structure — there's a reference page per builtin. And note that docs.rs reads BUILTIN_NAMES to insist each has a reference page. So adding a builtin requires a docs reference page. But again, no code changes needed for this spike.\n\nLet me quickly check the reference directory to understand builtin doc pages format.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ed132b3001R7W8H5YrE1uKiC","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","messageID":"msg_05ed103d6001MHIYfhcCSZpMdG"}}
