{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297216712,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a427f65e277bbb8c","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -n \"enum Type\\|Map\\|Record\\|Tuple\\|Vec\\|Stream\\|Opt\" src/ty.rs | head -30"},"output":"1:use std::collections::HashMap;\n6:/// back to itself through a `Vec`, that occurrence carries a placeholder in place of the real\n11:pub type Enums = HashMap<String, Type>;\n14:pub enum Type {\n22:    /// `Opt`: not because a 64-bit integer has no JSON spelling but because two backends\n33:    /// write one either); everywhere else -- function signatures, records, a `Vec<Char>` --\n36:    Vec(Box<Type>),\n45:    Record(Vec<(String, Type)>),\n48:    /// and only there -- the type grammar refuses it inside a Vec, a record, or another Stream\n49:    /// -- born at a source (`lines` is `Stream<Str>`), consumed exactly once per binding, and\n51:    Stream(Box<Type>),\n54:    /// the same way a `Stream` is -- a sink is not a value, so it never sits inside a Vec, a\n55:    /// record, a Stream, or an enum payload, and it is never a parameter -- with the position\n77:        args: Vec<Type>,\n78:        variants: Vec<(String, Option<Type>)>,\n88:const RESERVED_TYPE_NAMES: [&str; 2] = [\"Vec\", \"Stream\"];\n96:/// `Opt` is no longer here: it is the prelude's enum, reached through the ordinary generic\n99:    matches!(name, \"Vec\" | \"Stream\")\n103:    pub fn from_name(name: &str) -> Option<Type> {\n115:    /// Whether `ty` could hold a stream anywhere inside it -- through a Vec or a record\n120:            Type::Stream(_) => true,\n121:            Type::Vec(t) => t.contains_stream(),\n122:            Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| t.contains_stream()),\n130:    /// constructed type may contain one; the type grammar refuses every spelling of `Vec<Sink>`,\n131:    /// a record field, a Stream of one, and an enum payload, and the checker's one general\n136:            Type::Vec(t) | Type::Stream(t) => t.contains_sink(),\n137:            Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| t.contains_sink()),\n148:    pub fn elem(&self) -> Option<&Type> {\n150:            Type::Vec(t) => Some(t),\n155:    pub fn field(&self, name: &str) -> Option<&Type> {\n","metadata":{"output":"1:use std::collections::HashMap;\n6:/// back to itself through a `Vec`, that occurrence carries a placeholder in place of the real\n11:pub type Enums = HashMap<String, Type>;\n14:pub enum Type {\n22:    /// `Opt`: not because a 64-bit integer has no JSON spelling but because two backends\n33:    /// write one either); everywhere else -- function signatures, records, a `Vec<Char>` --\n36:    Vec(Box<Type>),\n45:    Record(Vec<(String, Type)>),\n48:    /// and only there -- the type grammar refuses it inside a Vec, a record, or another Stream\n49:    /// -- born at a source (`lines` is `Stream<Str>`), consumed exactly once per binding, and\n51:    Stream(Box<Type>),\n54:    /// the same way a `Stream` is -- a sink is not a value, so it never sits inside a Vec, a\n55:    /// record, a Stream, or an enum payload, and it is never a parameter -- with the position\n77:        args: Vec<Type>,\n78:        variants: Vec<(String, Option<Type>)>,\n88:const RESERVED_TYPE_NAMES: [&str; 2] = [\"Vec\", \"Stream\"];\n96:/// `Opt` is no longer here: it is the prelude's enum, reached through the ordinary generic\n99:    matches!(name, \"Vec\" | \"Stream\")\n103:    pub fn from_name(name: &str) -> Option<Type> {\n115:    /// Whether `ty` could hold a stream anywhere inside it -- through a Vec or a record\n120:            Type::Stream(_) => true,\n121:            Type::Vec(t) => t.contains_stream(),\n122:            Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| t.contains_stream()),\n130:    /// constructed type may contain one; the type grammar refuses every spelling of `Vec<Sink>`,\n131:    /// a record field, a Stream of one, and an enum payload, and the checker's one general\n136:            Type::Vec(t) | Type::Stream(t) => t.contains_sink(),\n137:            Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| t.contains_sink()),\n148:    pub fn elem(&self) -> Option<&Type> {\n150:            Type::Vec(t) => Some(t),\n155:    pub fn field(&self, name: &str) -> Option<&Type> {\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -n \"enum Type\\|Map\\|Record\\|Tuple\\|Vec\\|Stream\\|Opt\" src/ty.rs | head -30","time":{"start":1788297216708,"end":1788297216711}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I have an excellent understanding of the source family vocabulary. This is the key precedent: the sources family is `input`, `inputs`, `lines`, and `dsv(delim)`. `dsv` is \"the parameterized member of the sources family\" — the precedent for a parameterized source. \n\nNow, my HTTP sugar design can be grounded directly in the sources family. The maintainer note in the board says \"consider a simple HTTP query builtin (like Python requests) as thin sugar over whatever each backend runtime already provides\". The strongest grounding is to treat HTTP as a *source* (a parameterized source like `dsv`), since the language's I/O vocabulary is entirely source-shaped, and HTTP naturally produces a body that is either one JSON document (like `input`) or a stream of JSON documents (like `inputs`/`jsonlines` — which is exactly the shape of streaming APIs and SSE/JSON-lines endpoints).\n\nNow let me finalize the design candidates. Let me think about 3 concrete candidates:\n\n**Candidate 1: `http(url)` as a parameterized source, body-as-JSON, returning parsed body like `input`.**\n- `http(\"https://api.example.com/users\")` → yields the response body as a parsed JSON value (the same way `input` parses stdin). If the endpoint is JSON-lines (streaming API), a variant `http` could return a stream.\n- For POST and headers/body, since functions are unary and a record is how several arguments travel, pass a record: `http({url: \"...\", method: \"POST\", headers: {...}, body: {...}})`.\n- Composes with everything downstream as an ordinary value: `http(url) | .[].name | ...`. That's the cleanest composition: the result is a value, exactly like `input`. No new response-object type.\n- Response status/headers: NOT exposed in the minimal cut, or exposed via a refusal/non-2xx → error (like a parse failure). This is the most \"thin sugar\" option — it treats HTTP purely as a data source and ignores the HTTP envelope, matching how `input` ignores the fact that stdin is a file descriptor.\n\n**Candidate 2: `fetch(url)` returning a full response record `{status, headers, body}`, mirroring how records carry several fields.**\n- The response record gives the full minimal requests surface: `{status: Int, headers: <map>, body: <parsed JSON>}`.\n- Composes by field access: `fetch(url).body | .[].name` or `.status`.\n- But the `body` field has unknown type — the checker would need a generic/unknown. In the language today, `input` gets typed by expected type (first use wins). A response record's `body` field can't be typed that way easily since it's inside a record. This is a real friction with the current checker. This candidate is more faithful to requests' full surface but is the least \"thin sugar\" and has checker friction (the `body` field's unknown type is like the rejected `parse(.)` problem — expected types don't flow into a field access).\n\n**Candidate 3: two-entry-point, body-sugar plus status escape hatch** — `get(url)` for the common case (parsed body, like `input`) and something for status. Or reuse the `Result`/`Opt` vocabulary: HTTP returns `Result<Body, {status, error}>` — non-2xx becomes an `err` with the status. This composes with the existing `Result`/`Opt` enum vocabulary and the matcher.\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider. The task says \"each grounded in what the current sink/source vocabulary already supports (not a green-field invention) -- explain how each composes with existing pipe/sink syntax.\"\n\nSo the candidates should be grounded in the existing sink/source vocabulary. The strongest is the sources-family grounding (`dsv` precedent). Let me make three candidates that differ in how much of the HTTP envelope they expose:\n\n1. **`http(url)` as a body-only source** (most sugar, least surface): returns parsed JSON body like `input`; non-2xx or non-JSON is a refusal (runtime error like div-by-zero). No status/headers/body request in the minimal cut (or a record arg for those). Cleanest composition — it's literally a source.\n\n2. **`fetch(url)` as a response-record source** (full minimal surface): returns `{status, headers, body}`. Faithful to requests, but `body`'s unknown type is checker friction, and every use needs `.body`.\n\n3. **`http(url)` returning `Result<Body, {status, msg}>`** — folds status into the error side, keeps the happy path body-typed-like-`input`, composes with the matcher/`Result` vocabulary, exposes status only on failure.\n\nActually, I think the most grounded and honest recommendation is a combination: **HTTP as a parameterized source in the `dsv` family, defaulting to body-as-`input` semantics (Candidate 1), with a record argument when you need POST/headers/body, and status folded into a `Result`-typed error (Candidate 3's error folding) rather than a separate response-object type (Candidate 2's friction)**. \n\nBut the task explicitly wants 2-3 candidates presented, with a recommendation. So I'll present 3 candidates and recommend a synthesis: the body-only source as the default (`http(...)` behaves like `input`), with the response envelope surfaced only on the error path via `Result`, and note that Candidate 2's full response record is what requests does but is the least \"thin\" and has the strongest checker friction.\n\nNow let me nail down the syntax for the record argument given toylang's conventions. Since functions are unary and a record is how several arguments travel, and argument parens are optional when the argument is a record literal. So `http {url: ..., method: ..., headers: ..., body: ...}` or `http({...})`. \n\nActually wait — is `http` a builtin or a source keyword? Sources are keywords (`input`, `lines`, `dsv`). `dsv` is a keyword (Tok::Dsv) but takes a string-literal argument: `dsv(\";\")`. So `http(\"...\")` with a string literal URL would be the parameterized-source spelling that matches `dsv(\";\")` exactly. And for the full record form, since a record is how several arguments travel, `http({url: ..., method: ..., headers: ..., body: ...})`.\n\nHmm, but can a source take a record argument? `dsv` is restricted to \"a string literal, as in `dsv(\\\",\\\")`\". That's a parse-level restriction. The HTTP source is more complex — it needs headers (a record/map of strings) and a body (arbitrary JSON) and a method. That's richer than `dsv`'s single string literal. But the same *principle* — a parameterized source whose argument configures it — is the grounding.\n\nLet me also reconsider: does the language even have a way to build a map/record of arbitrary string->string for headers? Records are the JSON object shape `{...}`, and headers is `{Str: Str}`... The type grammar: a record `{Authorization: Str, ...}`. But headers have arbitrary keys — a map. Does toylang have a `Map`/dict type, or only records (closed, typed fields)? From what I've seen, records are closed field sets (a declared struct in Go). Headers with arbitrary keys wouldn't fit a closed record well — but for a fixed set of known headers you could write `{Authorization: \"...\", \"Content-Type\": \"...\"}` as a record. Actually the type `{Str: Str}` as a map might not exist. Let me check if there's a map type. From ty.rs, types are Int, Int64, Str, Char, Bool, Opt, Vec, Stream, records (field-name-keyed), enums. No general map keyed by arbitrary strings. So headers as `{Str: Str}` map is NOT supported by the current type system — headers would have to be a closed record of known header names, which is awkward for arbitrary headers.\n\nThis is an important finding: toylang has no general map/dict type, so arbitrary request headers (and response headers) don't fit the type system as-is. The minimal realistic surface would either (a) restrict headers to a closed record of a few known keys, or (b) represent headers as a `Vec<{name: Str, value: Str}>` (list of pairs) — which composes with the existing `Vec`/record vocabulary! A `Vec<{name, value}>` is fully expressible today. That's a nice grounded answer: headers as a list of name/value records, since there's no map type.\n\nActually, let me verify there's no map type. Let me check ty.rs quickly.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ed1ffd00013axo5X8m7YYFil","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","messageID":"msg_05ed142e5001Wq7GysRyUMGc2m"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297217101,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-bac9cd604e756e11","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"sed -n '/pub enum Type/,/^}/p' src/ty.rs"},"output":"pub enum Type {\n    Str,\n    Int,\n    /// A signed 64-bit integer that wraps, `Int`'s rules at twice the width (kantord/toylang#83,\n    /// extending ADR 0006). Deliberately a separate type rather than a widening of `Int`:\n    /// nothing converts implicitly, so mixing the two demands an explicit `i64(x)`. Literals\n    /// carry no suffix -- one that fits `Int` is `Int`, and any literal resolves as `Int64`\n    /// wherever one is expected, the `[]` rule applied to numbers. Barred from `input`, like\n    /// `Opt`: not because a 64-bit integer has no JSON spelling but because two backends\n    /// cannot yet read one faithfully (JS parses numbers into doubles), and that codec design\n    /// has not been done.\n    Int64,\n    Bool,\n    /// A single Unicode scalar value (kantord/toylang#75): never a surrogate half, so a\n    /// codepoint outside the Basic Multilingual Plane is one `Char`, on every backend, even\n    /// where a backend's own strings need a surrogate pair to spell it. Produced only by\n    /// `chars`, which is also the only way one is compared against a boundary -- there is no\n    /// literal syntax, so a program spells `'a'` as `chars(\"a\")[0]!`. Barred from `input` (no\n    /// wire form to read one from) and from the program's own printed result (no wire form to\n    /// write one either); everywhere else -- function signatures, records, a `Vec<Char>` --\n    /// it is an ordinary type.\n    Char,\n    Vec(Box<Type>),\n    /// Fields in declaration order. Order is not part of the type's identity (kantord/toylang#60:\n    /// `{a: Int, b: Int}` and `{b: Int, a: Int}` are one type, so `PartialEq` below compares the\n    /// field set, not the spelling), but it survives as metadata on every concrete value: a\n    /// literal's own written order is what it prints in on every backend and what the\n    /// native/Go column layouts key on. Whichever type a value is checked against is the order\n    /// it ends up carrying (`check::reorder_record` rebuilds a value that arrives shuffled), so\n    /// two differently-ordered values only coexist when nothing ever placed them in the same\n    /// checked position.\n    Record(Vec<(String, Type)>),\n    /// Effect-layer multiplicity: the entries arrive one at a time as evaluation proceeds, and\n    /// no stream object ever exists as a value (ADR 0001). Spellable in function signatures,\n    /// and only there -- the type grammar refuses it inside a Vec, a record, or another Stream\n    /// -- born at a source (`lines` is `Stream<Str>`), consumed exactly once per binding, and\n    /// exiting only through `collect`.\n    Stream(Box<Type>),\n    /// A sink: an expression that writes rather than producing a value, of which the only\n    /// instance today is the `jsonlines` builtin (gh:138 ruling). Second-class by construction,\n    /// the same way a `Stream` is -- a sink is not a value, so it never sits inside a Vec, a\n    /// record, a Stream, or an enum payload, and it is never a parameter -- with the position\n    /// rules stated once generally in the checker rather than hand-checked per builtin: a\n    /// `Sink` is legal only as the program's outermost expression or as a `Sink`-returning\n    /// function's body. A function may declare it as its return type and nothing else.\n    Sink,\n    /// A declared enum: nominal, so the name is the identity. A name (and, for a generic enum,\n    /// its arguments) determines the variants within a program, so `PartialEq` below compares\n    /// only those and not `variants` itself -- which matters because a self-referential enum's\n    /// own payload carries a placeholder here in place of the real list\n    /// (`check::types::resolve_named`, kantord/toylang#76) rather than the list every other\n    /// occurrence of the same name carries. A variant's payload is `None` for a unit variant,\n    /// in declaration order.\n    ///\n    /// The list riding along is a convenience, not the source of truth: read it and a\n    /// placeholder reads as an enum with no variants at all, which is how a nested recursive\n    /// value used to reach every backend's printer as something it could not render\n    /// (kantord/toylang#94). Ask `variants` instead.\n    Enum {\n        name: String,\n        /// The type arguments this instantiation was built with, in declaration order; empty\n        /// for an enum declared without parameters. Part of the identity: `Pair<Int>` and\n        /// `Pair<Str>` are different types even though both are `Pair`.\n        args: Vec<Type>,\n        variants: Vec<(String, Option<Type>)>,\n    },\n    /// A type parameter inside a generic enum's registry template, standing for whatever a\n    /// use site will supply. Never the type of any checked expression: instantiation\n    /// substitutes every one away before a type leaves resolution.\n    Param(String),\n}\n","metadata":{"output":"pub enum Type {\n    Str,\n    Int,\n    /// A signed 64-bit integer that wraps, `Int`'s rules at twice the width (kantord/toylang#83,\n    /// extending ADR 0006). Deliberately a separate type rather than a widening of `Int`:\n    /// nothing converts implicitly, so mixing the two demands an explicit `i64(x)`. Literals\n    /// carry no suffix -- one that fits `Int` is `Int`, and any literal resolves as `Int64`\n    /// wherever one is expected, the `[]` rule applied to numbers. Barred from `input`, like\n    /// `Opt`: not because a 64-bit integer has no JSON spelling but because two backends\n    /// cannot yet read one faithfully (JS parses numbers into doubles), and that codec design\n    /// has not been done.\n    Int64,\n    Bool,\n    /// A single Unicode scalar value (kantord/toylang#75): never a surrogate half, so a\n    /// codepoint outside the Basic Multilingual Plane is one `Char`, on every backend, even\n    /// where a backend's own strings need a surrogate pair to spell it. Produced only by\n    /// `chars`, which is also the only way one is compared against a boundary -- there is no\n    /// literal syntax, so a program spells `'a'` as `chars(\"a\")[0]!`. Barred from `input` (no\n    /// wire form to read one from) and from the program's own printed result (no wire form to\n    /// write one either); everywhere else -- function signatures, records, a `Vec<Char>` --\n    /// it is an ordinary type.\n    Char,\n    Vec(Box<Type>),\n    /// Fields in declaration order. Order is not part of the type's identity (kantord/toylang#60:\n    /// `{a: Int, b: Int}` and `{b: Int, a: Int}` are one type, so `PartialEq` below compares the\n    /// field set, not the spelling), but it survives as metadata on every concrete value: a\n    /// literal's own written order is what it prints in on every backend and what the\n    /// native/Go column layouts key on. Whichever type a value is checked against is the order\n    /// it ends up carrying (`check::reorder_record` rebuilds a value that arrives shuffled), so\n    /// two differently-ordered values only coexist when nothing ever placed them in the same\n    /// checked position.\n    Record(Vec<(String, Type)>),\n    /// Effect-layer multiplicity: the entries arrive one at a time as evaluation proceeds, and\n    /// no stream object ever exists as a value (ADR 0001). Spellable in function signatures,\n    /// and only there -- the type grammar refuses it inside a Vec, a record, or another Stream\n    /// -- born at a source (`lines` is `Stream<Str>`), consumed exactly once per binding, and\n    /// exiting only through `collect`.\n    Stream(Box<Type>),\n    /// A sink: an expression that writes rather than producing a value, of which the only\n    /// instance today is the `jsonlines` builtin (gh:138 ruling). Second-class by construction,\n    /// the same way a `Stream` is -- a sink is not a value, so it never sits inside a Vec, a\n    /// record, a Stream, or an enum payload, and it is never a parameter -- with the position\n    /// rules stated once generally in the checker rather than hand-checked per builtin: a\n    /// `Sink` is legal only as the program's outermost expression or as a `Sink`-returning\n    /// function's body. A function may declare it as its return type and nothing else.\n    Sink,\n    /// A declared enum: nominal, so the name is the identity. A name (and, for a generic enum,\n    /// its arguments) determines the variants within a program, so `PartialEq` below compares\n    /// only those and not `variants` itself -- which matters because a self-referential enum's\n    /// own payload carries a placeholder here in place of the real list\n    /// (`check::types::resolve_named`, kantord/toylang#76) rather than the list every other\n    /// occurrence of the same name carries. A variant's payload is `None` for a unit variant,\n    /// in declaration order.\n    ///\n    /// The list riding along is a convenience, not the source of truth: read it and a\n    /// placeholder reads as an enum with no variants at all, which is how a nested recursive\n    /// value used to reach every backend's printer as something it could not render\n    /// (kantord/toylang#94). Ask `variants` instead.\n    Enum {\n        name: String,\n        /// The type arguments this instantiation was built with, in declaration order; empty\n        /// for an enum declared without parameters. Part of the identity: `Pair<Int>` and\n        /// `Pair<Str>` are different types even though both are `Pair`.\n        args: Vec<Type>,\n        variants: Vec<(String, Option<Type>)>,\n    },\n    /// A type parameter inside a generic enum's registry template, standing for whatever a\n    /// use site will supply. Never the type of any checked expression: instantiation\n    /// substitutes every one away before a type leaves resolution.\n    Param(String),\n}\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"sed -n '/pub enum Type/,/^}/p' src/ty.rs","time":{"start":1788297217090,"end":1788297217097}},"id":"prt_05ed202c0001pVpCelgER9CRuz","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","messageID":"msg_05ed142e5001Wq7GysRyUMGc2m"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788297217121,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"id":"prt_05ed2045e001wRCIQ5hGRymevg","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"1da03e262f4e207599f9c8f9109adaed8aee925b","messageID":"msg_05ed142e5001Wq7GysRyUMGc2m","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":55298,"input":1281,"output":21,"reasoning":2028,"cache":{"write":0,"read":51968}},"cost":0.001283573}}
