{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297222747,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b37b3bbf9702bfe7","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cat docs/reference/builtins/jsonlines.md 2>/dev/null"},"output":"# jsonlines\n\n`jsonlines(v)`: prints each entry as JSON on its own line, instead of wrapping the whole\nthing in `[...]`. Named for the format (jsonlines.org, also called NDJSON). It takes a\n`Vec<T>` or a `Stream<T>`, and it is a sink: legal only as the program's outermost\nexpression, writing as it goes, with no result type, since nothing remains that could\nobserve one.\n\n```toylang\njsonlines([\"ada\", \"bo\", \"cy\"])\n```\n\n```output\n\"ada\"\n\"bo\"\n\"cy\"\n```\n\nEach entry is rendered as JSON, which is why the strings above are quoted where a top-level\n`Str` result would print raw.\n\nOver a stream, `jsonlines` is what makes a program incremental end to end: a\nread-one, transform-one, write-one loop that prints each line before the next is read,\nrather than holding stdin in memory. The corpus case pins the value; `tests/streaming.rs` is\nwhat proves output arrives before stdin closes.\n\n```case\njsonlines_of_inputs\n```\n\nBecause a sink has no result, `jsonlines` cannot appear inside a larger expression:\n\n```toylang\nlength(jsonlines([1, 2]))\n```\n\n```error\na sink is not a value, so it is legal only as the program's outermost expression or a Sink-returning function's body (at byte 7)\n```\n","metadata":{"output":"# jsonlines\n\n`jsonlines(v)`: prints each entry as JSON on its own line, instead of wrapping the whole\nthing in `[...]`. Named for the format (jsonlines.org, also called NDJSON). It takes a\n`Vec<T>` or a `Stream<T>`, and it is a sink: legal only as the program's outermost\nexpression, writing as it goes, with no result type, since nothing remains that could\nobserve one.\n\n```toylang\njsonlines([\"ada\", \"bo\", \"cy\"])\n```\n\n```output\n\"ada\"\n\"bo\"\n\"cy\"\n```\n\nEach entry is rendered as JSON, which is why the strings above are quoted where a top-level\n`Str` result would print raw.\n\nOver a stream, `jsonlines` is what makes a program incremental end to end: a\nread-one, transform-one, write-one loop that prints each line before the next is read,\nrather than holding stdin in memory. The corpus case pins the value; `tests/streaming.rs` is\nwhat proves output arrives before stdin closes.\n\n```case\njsonlines_of_inputs\n```\n\nBecause a sink has no result, `jsonlines` cannot appear inside a larger expression:\n\n```toylang\nlength(jsonlines([1, 2]))\n```\n\n```error\na sink is not a value, so it is legal only as the program's outermost expression or a Sink-returning function's body (at byte 7)\n```\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cat docs/reference/builtins/jsonlines.md 2>/dev/null","time":{"start":1788297222741,"end":1788297222744}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Very important finding: there is a `Sink` type already! \"A sink: an expression that writes rather than producing a value, of which the only instance today is the `jsonlines` builtin (gh:138 ruling).\" So the language already has a *sink* type concept. And there is NO general Map type — records are closed field sets.\n\nSo the vocabulary for HTTP:\n- Sources: `input`, `inputs`, `lines`, `dsv(delim)` — born outside the program, pull-based, typed by first use.\n- Sinks: `jsonlines` — writes a stream of values, the program's outermost expression. Only instance today.\n- No Map type → arbitrary headers can't be a `{Str: Str}`; they'd be a `Vec<{name, value}>` (list of pairs) or a closed record.\n\nThis is very relevant. The `Sink` type means HTTP could also be modeled as a sink for POST (writing a body), OR as a source for GET (reading a body). Both directions exist in the vocabulary. But a network request is really a query that returns a value, so GET-as-source is the natural fit.\n\nLet me read the jsonlines builtin reference and the Sink decision to understand the sink vocabulary.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ed21960001zDJoWMu13ZRyKq","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","messageID":"msg_05ed2046f001AyxuWUNIsZdJYB"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297222995,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-820f2d76f0b50b74","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -n \"Sink\\|jsonlines\\|sink\" draft.md | head -40"},"output":"1952:laziness would only buy memory, stopped being true when the fused `jsonlines(f(inputs))` loop\n1958:## DECIDED: `jsonlines`, and the jq tutorial reproduces in full\n1974:`jsonlines(v: Vec<T>) -> Str`, for any printable `T`, joins each element's compact-JSON encoding\n1979:the same function that prints the program's own result; `jsonlines` is that function invoked\n1983:jsonlines(commits(input) | map {message: .commit.message, name: .commit.committer.name})\n1988:Finding the right spelling exposed a real bug rather than causing one: `jsonlines` was the first\n2044:grammar production; now they are ordinary identifiers, reserved by name the same way `jsonlines`\n2175:`jsonlines(f(inputs))` loop made programs stream for real, while the checker kept typing\n2176:`inputs` as `Vec<T>` and `jsonlines(...)` as `Str`. Whether a program streamed was decided by a\n2193:wrote, and the ruling (gh:127, ratified in the streams-and-sinks grill round) keeps the\n2199:rule -- born in the program's own body, single-use, dying at `collect` or `jsonlines`, never\n2215:`jsonlines`, which becomes a *sink*: legal only as the program's outermost expression, with no\n2237:open. Making `jsonlines` a top-level-only sink removes the placeholder answer without\n2252:tests are needed for what the type now forbids: a nested `jsonlines`, a twice-consumed stream,\n","metadata":{"output":"1952:laziness would only buy memory, stopped being true when the fused `jsonlines(f(inputs))` loop\n1958:## DECIDED: `jsonlines`, and the jq tutorial reproduces in full\n1974:`jsonlines(v: Vec<T>) -> Str`, for any printable `T`, joins each element's compact-JSON encoding\n1979:the same function that prints the program's own result; `jsonlines` is that function invoked\n1983:jsonlines(commits(input) | map {message: .commit.message, name: .commit.committer.name})\n1988:Finding the right spelling exposed a real bug rather than causing one: `jsonlines` was the first\n2044:grammar production; now they are ordinary identifiers, reserved by name the same way `jsonlines`\n2175:`jsonlines(f(inputs))` loop made programs stream for real, while the checker kept typing\n2176:`inputs` as `Vec<T>` and `jsonlines(...)` as `Str`. Whether a program streamed was decided by a\n2193:wrote, and the ruling (gh:127, ratified in the streams-and-sinks grill round) keeps the\n2199:rule -- born in the program's own body, single-use, dying at `collect` or `jsonlines`, never\n2215:`jsonlines`, which becomes a *sink*: legal only as the program's outermost expression, with no\n2237:open. Making `jsonlines` a top-level-only sink removes the placeholder answer without\n2252:tests are needed for what the type now forbids: a nested `jsonlines`, a twice-consumed stream,\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -n \"Sink\\|jsonlines\\|sink\" draft.md | head -40","time":{"start":1788297222987,"end":1788297222992}},"id":"prt_05ed21a4d001a2E6n7ppl2b3qc","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","messageID":"msg_05ed2046f001AyxuWUNIsZdJYB"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788297223012,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"id":"prt_05ed21b5f0019npwGbc7YNIDQh","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"1da03e262f4e207599f9c8f9109adaed8aee925b","messageID":"msg_05ed2046f001AyxuWUNIsZdJYB","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":57422,"input":3801,"output":96,"reasoning":277,"cache":{"write":0,"read":53248}},"cost":0.001166173}}
