{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297258052,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8b21f61d8a3ef82d","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"sed -n '40,70p' src/lib.rs"},"output":"    /// The backends that must agree on every corpus program. Native and Rust each joined once\n    /// they could compile the whole language; until then they were kept out rather than allowed\n    /// to turn the harness permanently red, tracked by tests/backend_llvm.rs and\n    /// tests/backend_rust.rs respectively.\n    pub const ALL: [Backend; 7] = [\n        Backend::Lua,\n        Backend::Js,\n        Backend::Native,\n        Backend::Jq,\n        Backend::Go,\n        Backend::Py,\n        Backend::Rust,\n    ];\n\n    /// The spelling used on the command line and in a corpus case's `snapshot` list.\n    pub fn from_name(name: &str) -> Option<Backend> {\n        Backend::ALL.into_iter().find(|b| b.name() == name)\n    }\n\n    pub fn name(self) -> &'static str {\n        match self {\n            Backend::Lua => \"lua\",\n            Backend::Js => \"js\",\n            Backend::Native => \"native\",\n            Backend::Jq => \"jq\",\n            Backend::Go => \"go\",\n            Backend::Py => \"py\",\n            Backend::Rust => \"rust\",\n        }\n    }\n\n","metadata":{"output":"    /// The backends that must agree on every corpus program. Native and Rust each joined once\n    /// they could compile the whole language; until then they were kept out rather than allowed\n    /// to turn the harness permanently red, tracked by tests/backend_llvm.rs and\n    /// tests/backend_rust.rs respectively.\n    pub const ALL: [Backend; 7] = [\n        Backend::Lua,\n        Backend::Js,\n        Backend::Native,\n        Backend::Jq,\n        Backend::Go,\n        Backend::Py,\n        Backend::Rust,\n    ];\n\n    /// The spelling used on the command line and in a corpus case's `snapshot` list.\n    pub fn from_name(name: &str) -> Option<Backend> {\n        Backend::ALL.into_iter().find(|b| b.name() == name)\n    }\n\n    pub fn name(self) -> &'static str {\n        match self {\n            Backend::Lua => \"lua\",\n            Backend::Js => \"js\",\n            Backend::Native => \"native\",\n            Backend::Jq => \"jq\",\n            Backend::Go => \"go\",\n            Backend::Py => \"py\",\n            Backend::Rust => \"rust\",\n        }\n    }\n\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"sed -n '40,70p' src/lib.rs","time":{"start":1788297258048,"end":1788297258051}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I have a complete picture of the sink/source vocabulary. Let me read the jsonlines decision a bit more for the sink concept, and the streams-and-sinks section 2175-2260. Actually, I have enough. Let me also quickly confirm the examples directory for a full-program example style, and check what a \"reference\" example looks like for a source (e.g., how dsv is written in a program).\n\nLet me also survey the Python requests minimal surface from knowledge (I know it well):\n- `requests.get(url, params=None, headers=None)` -> `Response`\n- `requests.post(url, data=None, json=None, headers=None)` -> `Response`\n- `Response.status_code` (int), `Response.headers` (case-insensitive dict), `Response.text` (str body), `Response.json()` (parsed JSON body).\n- `requests.get(...).raise_for_status()` raises on 4xx/5xx.\n\nThe minimal realistic surface: get, post, request headers, request body (data/json), response status code, response body (text and parsed JSON). That's the surface gh:171 lists (get/post? headers? body?).\n\nNow let me write the research file. Let me structure it:\n\n1. Context: the ask (gh:171, board row), what \"thin sugar\" means.\n2. What the vocabulary already is: sources family (`input`/`inputs`/`lines`/`dsv`), the sink concept (`jsonlines`), records-carry-arguments, no Map type, `refuses` for runtime failures, backends.\n3. What each backend provides (the survey the board asks for): Go net/http, JS fetch, Python urllib (not requests — it's third party), Lua (nothing), jq (nothing), Rust backend (no external crates + no stdlib HTTP), native C (nothing). TLS: Go/Python/JS do https natively; Rust/native/Lua/jq would need external deps for TLS. → realistic target is Go/Python/JS.\n4. The requests surface, minimal.\n5. Three syntax candidates, each grounded:\n   - Candidate 1: `http(url)` as body-only parameterized source (dsv-family precedent), typed like `input` by first use; record argument for POST/headers/body; non-2xx/non-JSON is a runtime refusal. Composition: it IS a source, so everything downstream (`|`, `select`, `map`, `collect`, field access) works unchanged.\n   - Candidate 2: `fetch(url)` returning a response record `{status, headers, body}` — full requests surface, but `body`'s unknown type and no Map type for headers cause checker friction; least \"thin\".\n   - Candidate 3: `http(url)` typed `Result<Body, {status, msg}>` — folds status into the error path, keeps happy path body-typed-like-`input`, composes with the matcher and prelude `Result`/`Opt`. Body on the happy path is exactly `input`-shaped.\n6. Recommendation: Candidate 1 as the default (the thin sugar), with the error path folded in (borrowing Candidate 3's `Result`) rather than a response object (Candidate 2), because it's the only one that composes with the existing source vocabulary without inventing a new response type or a Map type, and it matches how `input` already ignores the stdin file descriptor / only surfaces parse failures. Note the open questions (headers representation, which backends, TLS, whether GET is a source and POST a sink).\n\nLet me be careful to keep it grounded and honest, matching the repo's prose style (AGENTS.md writing rules: plain ASCII, name things, asymmetry of detail, no boilerplate, no invented APIs, provenance).\n\nLet me verify a couple facts before writing:\n- Does toylang have a way to write a record literal with fields? Yes — `{message: ..., name: ...}` appears in draft.md examples. And record is how several arguments travel. Good.\n- Are headers representable? No Map type, so `Vec<{name, value}>` is the grounded representation. Let me double check there's genuinely no map/dict type. From ty.rs Type enum: Str, Int, Int64, Bool, Char, Vec, Record, Stream, Sink, Enum, Param. No Map. Confirmed.\n\n- Backends that exist: go, jq, js, llvm(native), lua, py, rs, toylang(?). Let me check lib.rs for the backend list / CLI.\n\nLet me confirm backend names.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ed2a4160011uD4fLNouIqV6u","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","messageID":"msg_05ed21b700014x54H6wO7X1qt5"}}
