{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297276130,"sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-93dad5d650abe80c","state":{"status":"error","input":{"command":"which go node python3 jq cc rustc 2>/dev/null; echo \"---node version---\"; node --version 2>/dev/null; echo \"---python---\"; python3 --version 2>/dev/null; echo \"---go---\"; go version 2>/dev/null"},"error":"The user rejected permission to use this specific tool call.","time":{"start":1788297276124,"end":1788297276129}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Seven backends: Lua, Js, Native, Jq, Go, Py, Rust. (There's also a `toylang` emitter — emit_toylang.rs — but it's a formatter/self-hosted target, not in ALL; it's for fmt.)\n\nNow I have all the facts I need. Let me write the research file. Let me draft the content carefully following AGENTS.md writing rules.\n\nKey findings to include:\n1. **What the vocabulary already is.** Sources family (input/inputs/lines/dsv), the `dsv(delim)` parameterized-source precedent, the `Sink` type (jsonlines), records-as-arguments, no Map type, `refuses` for runtime-only failure, the \"output needs no side effect\" decision. This is the grounding.\n\n2. **What each backend provides** (the survey the board asks for):\n   - Go: net/http (http.Get, NewRequest, Client.Do; resp.StatusCode/Header/Body). https native.\n   - JS (node): global fetch (Node 18+); resp.status/.headers/.text()/.json(). https native.\n   - Python: urllib.request (stdlib) — requests itself is third-party, not shipped; the \"like requests\" is a UX reference, not a target. https via ssl stdlib.\n   - Lua: no stdlib network; LuaSocket is an external C dep. mlua embeds; nothing available.\n   - jq: no network builtins in jq 1.7.\n   - Rust backend: emits one self-contained file, no external crates (explicit constraint); stdlib has no HTTP; TLS impossible without a dep.\n   - Native (LLVM/C): hand-rolled; no HTTP; TLS needs a lib.\n\n   So the honest target set is Go, Py, JS (all do https with zero new deps). Lua, jq, native, Rust cannot without either external deps or limiting to plain-http. This is the \"which backend(s) it targets\" answer.\n\n3. **The requests minimal surface.** get/post, request headers, request body, response status, response body (text + parsed JSON). And the composition.\n\n4. **Three candidates.**\n\nLet me write it now. I'll be careful about detail asymmetry — the hard parts (the composition, the no-Map-type friction, the backend/TLS split) get paragraphs; the obvious gets a line.\n\nLet me also decide provenance: \n- The requests surface description is \"Derived\" from the requests library docs (external source). \n- The backend survey is \"Derived\" from the repo (existing code) + my knowledge of Go/JS/Python stdlibs — I should note that the network capability claims for Go/Python/JS stdlibs are from knowledge, not verified against installed toolchains. Actually the AGENTS says \"Never invent an API signature. If you can't verify a call, say the call is unverified.\" I haven't verified Go's net/http, Node's fetch, urllib, LuaSocket in this environment. I should mark those as unverified knowledge or verify them. Let me quickly verify what's available in the environment (do we have go, node, python?). Actually the claims about these libraries are well-established public API knowledge, not invented. But to be safe per AGENTS.md, I'll state the ones I'm confident about and flag the ones I haven't run. Actually the pattern of research files (erlang-target-research.md) says \"Everything below is a documented-semantics comparison; no Erlang toolchain was consulted or run.\" So I can similarly note that the backend survey is documented-semantics comparison, not run.\n\nLet me quickly check what tools are available, cheaply, to be accurate.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ed2e8e7001OqdaVkzzCkXDZe","sessionID":"ses_fa130e14bffeHOCZtybDeHH22K","messageID":"msg_05ed2a577001azvhhZRPJoXnN0"}}
